= Topology
{wiki}

Topology studies <topological spaces> through <open sets> and <continuous maps>. Its central equivalence is a <homeomorphism>: a <bijection> that preserves <continuity> in both directions. This lets us distinguish spaces by <connectedness> or <compactness> even when numerical <metric> values are irrelevant. The <open interval> $(0,1)$ and the <real line> have the same <topology> in this sense, although only the former is a <bounded set> with its usual <metric>.

\Image[topological-continuity-examples.png]
{title=Homeomorphisms, inverse images, and the effect of removing a point}

The upper panels show an explicit <homeomorphism> and the <inverse image> test for a <continuous map>. The lower panels exhibit an obstruction to a <homeomorphism>: removing any point from a <circle> leaves a <connected space>, whereas removing an interior point from a <closed interval> gives two <connected components>.

<Homotopy> is a different notion: it varies a <continuous map> continuously with a parameter and may collapse points. For example, $H(x,t)=(1-t)x$ contracts $[0,1]$ to zero. Thus this <closed interval> and a <singleton> have the same <homotopy type>, but there is no <homeomorphism> between them because there is no <bijection>. <Homotopy equivalence> need not preserve <compactness>. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-905-algebraic-topology-i-fall-2016/2dad3dd8354992d877fbeca4049817ee_MIT18_905F16_lecture_notes.pdf[MIT Algebraic Topology I, Lecture 5]{ref}

The abstract viewpoint already appears explicitly in Tietze's 1923 paper: he describes <topology> as the study of properties invariant under invertible <continuous maps> with <continuous> inverses, and discusses axioms for <neighbourhoods>, citing Hausdorff's 1914 book, page 213. https://gdz.sub.uni-goettingen.de/fulltext/PPN235181684_0088/00000294?_format=xml[Tietze, Beiträge zur allgemeinen Topologie I, page 290]{ref}

= Initial topology
{parent=Topology}
{title2=$\sigma(f_i:i\in I)$}

For a family of <functions> $f_i:X\to Y_i$ into <topological spaces>, the <initial topology> on $X$ is the coarsest <topology> making them all <continuous>. Its <subbasis of a topology>[subbasis] consists of $f_i^{-1}(U)$ for open <subsets> $U\subseteq Y_i$. A map into $X$ is <continuous> exactly when all its composites with the $f_i$ are <continuous>.

= Initial topology of separately continuous functions
{parent=Initial topology}
{title2=$\mathcal T_{\rm sc}$}

On the real plane, take the <initial topology> of all <separately continuous> real-valued <functions>. Its coordinate lines have their usual induced <topology>, and the nonzero rational grid is <dense>, but its diagonal is <uncountable> and discrete. Thus the space is <separable topological space>[separable] and nonmetrizable. Rectangular bump <functions> show that no <sequence> from the nonzero rational grid converges to the origin, so the space is not <first countable> either.

= Topology with countable closed sets away from one point
{parent=Topology}

Fix a point $a$ of an uncountable set $X$ and declare $F$ closed when $a\in F$ or $F$ is countable. Finite unions and arbitrary intersections satisfy the closed-set axioms. Every convergent sequence is eventually constant: the countable set of its values different from the limit is closed, so its complement is a neighborhood of the limit. A real-valued map constant at $f(a)$ outside a countable set is continuous, because each closed inverse image either contains $a$ or lies in that countable exceptional set.

= Zero-dimensional space
{parent=Topology}

A space is zero-dimensional when its topology has a base of <clopen sets>. In the Hausdorff setting these sets separate distinct points, so the space is <totally disconnected>. Conversely a compact Hausdorff <totally disconnected space> has a clopen base, by the <Šura-Bura component lemma> and finite subcovers of the complement of a neighborhood.

= Clopen evaluation embedding
{parent=Zero-dimensional space}
{title2=$e(x)=(\mathbf1_U(x))_{U\in\mathcal B}$}

The <characteristic functions> of a <clopen> <topological basis> give a <topological embedding> of a zero-dimensional $T_0$ <topological space> into a product of discrete two-point <topological spaces>. The <separation> axiom makes those <functions> distinguish points, and their coordinate preimages recover the original <topological basis>.

= Strong zero-dimensionality
{parent=Zero-dimensional space}

In a normal Hausdorff space, strong zero-dimensionality means that any disjoint closed sets can be separated by a <clopen set>, equivalently by disjoint clopen neighborhoods. For general <Tychonoff spaces>, the usual definition asks this for completely separated sets. In the normal setting <Urysohn's lemma> makes the formulations agree. The condition is equivalent to zero-dimensionality of the <Stone-Čech compactification>.

= Strong zero-dimensionality and Stone-Čech compactification
{parent=Strong zero-dimensionality}
{title2=$X\text{ strongly zero-dimensional}\iff\beta X\text{ zero-dimensional}$}

For normal Hausdorff $X$, if $\beta X$ has a clopen base, extend a <Urysohn's lemma> separator of two closed subsets of $X$. Their closures in $\beta X$ are separated; compactness gives a clopen separator, whose trace works in $X$. Conversely separate the traces of the low and high level sets of a continuous $f:\beta X\to[0,1]$ by clopen $U\subseteq X$. The characteristic function of $U$ extends into $\{0,1\}$ on $\beta X$. Density ensures its one level contains the zero level of $f$ and misses the one level. Applying this with $f$ separating a point from a closed set gives a clopen base for $\beta X$.

= Hausdorff compactification
{parent=Topology}
{title2=$j:X\hookrightarrow K$}

A Hausdorff compactification is a compact <Hausdorff space> $K$ with a <topological embedding> $j:X\to K$ whose image is dense. It retains the original topology on $X$ while adjoining boundary points. For a locally compact noncompact Hausdorff space, its <one-point compactification> is an example. For every <Tychonoff space>, the <Stone-Čech compactification> extends all continuous maps into compact Hausdorff targets.

= Locally compact subspaces are open in Hausdorff compactifications
{parent=Hausdorff compactification}

If a locally <compact> <Hausdorff space> is densely embedded in a <Hausdorff space>, its image is <open>. Around a point choose an ambient <open set> whose trace lies in a <compact> <neighborhood> $K$. Density puts that ambient <open set> inside the ambient <closure> of its trace, which lies in $K$ because <compact subsets> of a <Hausdorff space> are <closed>. Thus the ambient <open set> lies in the image.

= Two ends of a real-line compactification
{parent=Hausdorff compactification}
{title2=$Y\setminus h[\mathbb R]=C^+\cup C^-$}

For a <Hausdorff compactification> $h:\mathbb R\to Y$, let $C^\pm$ be the intersections of the closures of the positive and negative tails, respectively. Each is nonempty and <connected> by the closed-filter-base theorem. Their <union> equals the remainder: an image point has a bounded-interval <neighbourhood> excluding sufficiently long tails, whereas every <neighbourhood> of a remainder point meets tails by density and <compactness> of bounded intervals. If the remainder is finite, each $C^\pm$ must be a <singleton>, so there can be only one or two remainder points. In particular there is no three-point compactification of the <real line>.

= Čech-complete space
{c}
{parent=Hausdorff compactification}

A <Tychonoff space> is Čech-complete if it is a <G-delta set> in a <Hausdorff compactification>. The <G-delta compactification invariance> makes this independent of the chosen compactification, and equivalent to being a <G-delta set> in its <Stone-Čech compactification>.

= Čech-complete spaces are Baire spaces
{c}
{parent=Čech-complete space}

Write the dense space $X$ as $\bigcap_nG_n$ in compact Hausdorff $K$. Every $G_n$ is dense and open. A dense open subset $U_n$ of $X$ has the form $X\cap O_n$ with $O_n$ dense and open in $K$. The compact <Baire category theorem> makes $\bigcap_n(G_n\cap O_n)=\bigcap_nU_n$ dense in $K$, hence in $X$. Therefore every <Čech-complete space> is a <Baire space>. The rationals are not a <Baire space>, as deleting their points one at a time gives dense open sets with empty intersection.

= G-delta compactification invariance
{parent=Čech-complete space}

For the comparison map $\pi:\beta X\to K$, the <fibers of compactification comparison maps> identity $\pi^{-1}(X)=X$ pulls a <G-delta set> presentation of $X$ back from $K$. Conversely if $\beta X\setminus X=\bigcup_nF_n$ with $F_n$ closed compact, then $K\setminus X=\bigcup_n\pi(F_n)$. Each image is closed and misses $X$. Thus $X$ is a <G-delta set> in one <Hausdorff compactification> exactly when it is in every one.

= Fibers of compactification comparison maps
{parent=Hausdorff compactification}
{title2=$\pi^{-1}(X)=X$}

If $\pi:\beta X\to K$ is the canonical map from the <Stone-Čech compactification> onto a <Hausdorff compactification>, its fibers over the original points are singletons. If $\pi(z)=x\in X$ and $z\ne x$, choose an open neighborhood $U$ of $x$ in $\beta X$ whose closure excludes $z$. Choose open $V\subseteq K$ with $x\in V$ and $V\cap X\subseteq U$. The open neighborhood $\pi^{-1}V$ of $z$ has dense trace $V\cap X$, placing $z$ in $\overline U$, a contradiction. This controls when images of closed remainder sets stay in the remainder.

= Finer topology
{parent=Topology}
{title2=$\tau\subseteq\sigma$}

For two <topologies> on the same <set> $X$, $\sigma$ is finer than $\tau$ when every $\tau$-<open set> is $\sigma$-<open>. Equivalently, the <identity function> $(X,\sigma)\to(X,\tau)$ is <continuous>, by the definition using <preimages> of <open sets>. The inclusion may be an equality; strictly finer means proper inclusion.

= Topological disjoint union
{parent=Topology}
{title2=$\coprod_{i\in I}X_i$}

= Disjoint union topology
{synonym}

The topological disjoint union of <topological spaces> $X_i$ has the labelled <disjoint union> as its underlying <set>. A <subset> is <open> exactly when its <intersection> with each labelled copy $X_i$ is <open> in that copy. Each copy is a <clopen set>, and a <function> from the topological disjoint union is <continuous> exactly when its <restriction> to every copy is <continuous>. This follows immediately by testing <preimages> of <open sets> on each copy. A <topological space> is <homeomorphic> to the topological disjoint union of its <connected components> exactly when all those <connected components> are <open>.

= Topological invariant
{parent=Topology}

A topological invariant is a property or mathematical object preserved by <homeomorphisms> of <topological spaces>. <Connectedness> and <compactness> are examples. The <fundamental group> is an invariant up to <group isomorphism>; it is also preserved up to <isomorphism> by <homotopy equivalence>, a stronger invariance property which does not hold for every topological invariant.

= End
{disambiguate=topology}
{parent=Topology}
{wiki}

An end of a noncompact <topological space> describes a coherent way to escape every compact subset. For a cofinal compact exhaustion, when one exists, $K_1\subseteq K_2\subseteq\cdots$, it is represented by nested connected components of the complements. A connected noncompact manifold is <disconnected at infinity> when it has more than one end.

= Orientability
{parent=Topology}
{wiki}

Orientability asks whether local choices of handedness can agree everywhere on a <manifold>. On a <smooth manifold>, an <orientation of a smooth manifold> chooses an <orientation of a vector space> for each <tangent space>, continuously with position. An <ordered basis> describes the same handedness as another one when the change-of-basis <determinant> is positive. An <oriented atlas> implements this condition through positive <Jacobian determinants> of the <smooth transition maps>.

A paper strip makes the obstruction visible. Join its short ends without twisting: the resulting <untwisted band> admits a consistent choice of <unit normal>. Add a half-turn before joining: on the <Möbius band>, following the <core circle of a Möbius band> reverses that choice when you first return to the same point. A local decision about handedness has encountered a global contradiction. The <normal reversal around the Möbius core> makes this precise for an explicit model.

\Image[orientability-cylinder-mobius-edge-gluing.png]
{title=Matching and reversed edge identifications on a cylinder and a Möbius band}

Both rectangles have coordinates $(u,v)\in[0,1]\times[-1,1]$. The <untwisted band> identifies $(0,v)$ with $(1,v)$; the <Möbius band> identifies $(0,v)$ with $(1,-v)$. Across a seam the corresponding local coordinate changes preserve or reverse $v$, giving <determinants> $+1$ and $-1$. This sign explains the handedness change; the drawing also tracks the two <boundary> circles of the <untwisted band> and the single <boundary> circle of the <Möbius band>.

For a <smooth> <topological surface> embedded in $\mathbb R^3$, choosing an <orientation of a surface> is equivalent to choosing a continuous <unit normal>. This picture is specific to surfaces in three-dimensional space: the general definition uses compatible local orientations, without requiring an <embedding>. <Orientability> is a <topological invariant>, so a <homeomorphism> cannot turn a <Möbius band> into a <untwisted band>. The <sphere> and <torus> are <orientable surfaces>; the <Klein bottle> is not. https://www.open.edu/openlearn/science-maths-technology/mathematics-statistics/surfaces/content-section-3.2[Open University, Surfaces, §3.2: orientability]{ref}

The distinction matters when a sign has geometric meaning. A signed <flux> <surface integral> needs an <orientation of a surface>, and <Stokes theorem> relates that choice to the <outward-normal-first boundary orientation>. Ordinary unsigned <surface area> does not require <orientability>. Thus a <Möbius band> still has a perfectly meaningful area even though it has no continuous global <unit normal>.

A <connected> orientable <manifold> of positive dimension has two possible orientations: reversing every local choice gives the other one. If a <manifold> has several <connected components>, each component can be oriented independently when it is orientable. For <topological surfaces>, a useful obstruction criterion is the presence of a <Möbius band>: a surface is nonorientable exactly when it contains such a band. https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~mec/Winter2009/Victor/part3.htm[Cornell Mathematical Explorers' Club, Orientable and Nonorientable Surfaces]{ref}

= Non-Hausdorff manifold
{parent=Topology}
{wiki}

A <non-Hausdorff manifold> is a locally Euclidean <topological space> for which distinct points need not have disjoint neighborhoods. The real line with two origins is a basic example. Nonseparated algebraic schemes exhibit analogous gluing phenomena, but are not generally topological manifolds.

= Line with two origins
{parent=Non-Hausdorff manifold}

Take $\mathbb R\times\{0,1\}$ with its <Euclidean topology> and identify $(t,0)$ with $(t,1)$ for every $t\neq0$, retaining two distinct origins. This <quotient space> is locally <homeomorphic> to the <real line> but is not <Hausdorff>. Any <open> <neighbourhoods> of its origins have <preimages> containing $(-\varepsilon,\varepsilon)\times\{0\}$ and $(-\delta,\delta)\times\{1\}$, so a nonzero $|t|<\min(\varepsilon,\delta)$ gives a shared image point. All <equivalence classes> in the original <metric space> are finite and <closed>. This shows that <closed> <equivalence classes> do not suffice for a <Hausdorff> <quotient space>.

= Stone space
{c}
{parent=Topology}
{wiki}

A <Stone space> is a <compact> <Hausdorff space> that is <totally disconnected>, equivalently a compact Hausdorff space with a basis of clopen sets. The topology on a <type space> gives an important example.

= Tail topology on the natural numbers
{parent=Topology}
{title2=$\tau=\{\varnothing\}\cup\{\{n,n+1,\ldots\}:n\in\mathbb N\}$}

The nonempty open sets are the upper tails of the <natural numbers>. Arbitrary nonempty unions select the smallest starting index, and finite intersections select the largest, so these sets form a <topology>. A map into this space is <continuous> exactly when the inverse image of every upper tail is open.

= Continuous maps between cofinite and tail topologies
{parent=Tail topology on the natural numbers}

For a map $f:\mathbb N\to\mathbb N$ from the <cofinite topology> to the <tail topology on the natural numbers>, <continuity> is equivalent to every set $\{n:f(n)\ge k\}$ being empty or cofinite. If the image is unbounded, every such set is nonempty and cofinite, so $f(n)\to\infty$. If it is bounded, its maximum $M$ exists, and the inverse image of the tail at $M$ forces $f(n)=M$ eventually, with $f(n)\le M$ always. Conversely either condition makes every tail preimage empty or cofinite.

= Fell topology
{parent=Topology}
{c}

The <Fell topology> on the closed subsets of a <locally compact space> has subbasic open sets requiring intersection with an open set or avoidance of a <compact set>. On a locally compact separable metric space, its sequential convergence says that every convergent subsequence of points from the varying sets has its limit in the limiting set, and every point of the limiting set is approximated by points from the varying sets.

= Upper-ray topology on the real line
{parent=Topology}
{title2=$\{\varnothing,\mathbb R\}\cup\{(a,\infty):a\in\mathbb R\}$}

These sets form a <topology>: a nonempty union of upper rays is another upper ray, or all of $\mathbb R$ when their endpoints have no lower bound; a finite intersection of rays has endpoint the maximum of their endpoints. The whole line must be included. The <continuous map> $n\mapsto n$ from $\mathbb N$ with the <cofinite topology> to this space is nonconstant, because each upper ray has cofinite inverse image.

= Compact subsets of the upper-ray topology
{parent=Upper-ray topology on the real line}

For the topology consisting of the empty set, the whole real line, and upper open rays, a nonempty subset is <compact> exactly when it has a smallest element. An open set containing the smallest element contains the entire subset. Conversely, if there is no smallest element, the rays $(a,\infty)$ indexed by points $a$ of the subset cover it, but any finite selection misses its smallest chosen endpoint.

= Hyperconnected space
{parent=Topology}

A <topological space> is hyperconnected if any two nonempty <open sets> intersect. Every <continuous map> from a nonempty hyperconnected space to a <Hausdorff space> is constant: two distinct image points would have disjoint open neighbourhoods whose nonempty inverse images contradict hyperconnectedness. Every infinite set with the <cofinite topology> is hyperconnected.

= Closed cover
{parent=Topology}
{title2=$X=\bigcup_iF_i,\qquad F_i\text{ closed}$}

A closed cover of a <topological space> is a family of <closed sets> whose union is the whole space. A finite closed cover can be used in separation and antipodal arguments: in a <metric space>, vanishing distance to a closed member means actual membership. Unlike an <open cover>, a closed cover does not automatically have a partition of unity subordinate to its members.

= Partition topology
{parent=Topology}

Given a partition of a <set>, declare a subset open precisely when it is a union of partition blocks. Arbitrary unions and finite intersections of such unions are again unions of blocks, giving a <topology>. The quotient map to the block set with its <discrete topology> pulls that topology back to the original set. For the partition of $\mathbb Z$ into its two parity classes, the only open sets are the empty set, those two classes, and all of $\mathbb Z$.

= Finite subsets do not define a topology on an infinite set
{parent=Topology}

On an infinite <set>, the family consisting of all finite subsets and the whole set need not be closed under arbitrary unions. On $\mathbb Z$, the union of the singleton subsets of $2\mathbb Z$ is infinite and proper, so this family is not a <topology>. It must not be confused with the <cofinite topology>, whose proper open sets have finite complements.

= Lower limit topology
{parent=Topology}
{wiki}

= Sorgenfrey line
{c}
{synonym}

The lower limit topology on $\mathbb R$ has <basis of a topology> $\{[a,b):a<b\}$. Every such nonempty interval contains a rational, so this <topological space> is separable. Its topology is finer than the usual Euclidean topology; the one-sided basic neighbourhoods allow its square to have an uncountable discrete antidiagonal.

= Sorgenfrey plane
{c}
{parent=Lower limit topology}
{wiki}

The Sorgenfrey plane is the <product topology> on two copies of the <Sorgenfrey line>. It is separable because $\mathbb Q^2$ meets every nonempty basic rectangle $[a,b)\times[c,d)$. Its antidiagonal $\{(x,-x):x\in\mathbb R\}$ is an uncountable discrete subspace: $[x,x+\epsilon)\times[-x,-x+\epsilon)$ meets it only at $(x,-x)$. In a discrete space every dense subset is the whole space, so this subspace is not separable. Thus separability need not pass to subspaces of nonmetrizable spaces.

// Target: analysis.bigb

= Symmetric product
{parent=Topology}
{title2=$\operatorname{Sym}^g(X)$}

The $g$th symmetric product of a space $X$ is $\operatorname{Sym}^g(X)=X^g/S_g$, where the <symmetric group> permutes the factors. Its points are unordered $g$-tuples, with repetitions allowed. If $X$ is a <Riemann surface>, elementary symmetric functions in local complex coordinates make its symmetric product a smooth complex manifold, including along the locus of repeated points. A collection of disjoint circles in $X$ gives a product torus in this symmetric product.

= Extremally disconnected space
{parent=Topology}

A <topological space> is extremally disconnected when the closure of every <open set> is open. This concerns open sets, not arbitrary subsets. In particular, a nonisolated singleton in a <Hausdorff space> is closed but not open, so its closure gives a counterexample to that stronger condition.

= Countable complement topology
{parent=Topology}
{wiki}

= Cocountable topology
{synonym}

The open sets are the empty set and subsets whose complement is countable. On an uncountable set, any two nonempty open sets intersect, so the space is not <Hausdorff>. On a countable underlying set this topology is discrete. The <compact subsets of a cocountable space> are finite, regardless of the cardinality of the underlying set.

= Compact subsets of a cocountable space
{parent=Countable complement topology}

Only finite subsets are compact in the <cocountable topology>. An infinite subset contains distinct $c_1,c_2,\ldots$. The open sets obtained by removing the tails $\{c_j:j\geq n\}$ form an increasing <open cover>, but every finite selection misses a tail point. Finite sets are compact in every topology.

= Locally finite family of subsets
{parent=Topology}
{title2=$(A_i)_{i\in I}$}

A family $(A_i)$ of subsets of a <topological space> is locally finite if every point has a neighbourhood meeting only finitely many of its members. On a compact space such a family has only finitely many nonempty members. For singleton subsets of a <Riemann surface>, this is the condition permitting prescribed poles of a <meromorphic function> without accumulation inside the surface.

= Indiscrete topology
{parent=Topology}
{title2=$\{\varnothing,X\}$}

The indiscrete topology on a set $X$ has only the empty set and the whole set as <open sets>. When $X$ has more than one point it is not a <Hausdorff space>. For example the <quotient topology> on $\mathbb R/\mathbb Q$ is indiscrete: a nonempty open saturated subset contains an interval and all its rational translates, which cover $\mathbb R$.

= Triangulation of a surface
{parent=Topology}

A triangulation of a surface decomposes it into triangles meeting along full edges or vertices. On a closed surface, each edge is incident with two triangle faces, so $3F=2E$. <Euler characteristic> connects this local counting relation with the surface's topology.

= Pasting lemma for closed subspaces
{parent=Topology}

= Pasting lemma
{synonym}

If a <topological space> is the union of finitely many <closed subspaces> and <continuous maps> on those subspaces agree on every overlap, they combine into a <continuous map> on the whole space. For a <closed set> in the target, its <preimage> is a <finite union> of <closed sets>, proving continuity.

= Cofinite topology
{parent=Topology}
{wiki}
{title2=$\tau_{\mathrm{cof}}=\{\varnothing\}\cup\{X\setminus F:F\subseteq X\text{ finite}\}$}

= Finite complement topology
{synonym}

The <cofinite topology> on a <set> $X$ declares the <empty set> and all <cofinite> <subsets> to be <open sets>: a nonempty <open set> may exclude only a <finite set> of points. There is no assumed <metric>. On an <infinite set>, this gives a particularly small collection of <open sets> while still making every <singleton> a <closed set>. On a <finite set>, every <subset> has finite <complement>, so the <cofinite topology> is the <discrete topology>.<Point-Set Topology by Romyar Sharifi/Chapter 1: Topological spaces>[Definition 1.1.9: finite complement topology]{ref}

For an original example, use $X=\mathbb N_{>0}$ and choose
$$
U=X\setminus\{2,5,8\},\qquad V=X\setminus\{1,3,7\}.
$$
Both are <open sets>, and their <intersection> is $U\cap V=X\setminus\{1,2,3,5,7,8\}$. Thus $4,6,9,10,\ldots$ are in both. Their finite exclusions cannot separate the infinite remainder. The <finite intersection> of any finite collection of nonempty <open sets> is again a nonempty <open set>, since its <complement> is a <finite union> of <finite sets>. An arbitrary nonempty <union> is <open> because its <complement> is contained in the finite <complement> of any one nonempty member.

\Image[cofinite-open-sets-finite-exclusions.png]
{title=Two <open sets> with finite exclusions still overlap in an infinite <cofinite topology>}
{description=Original sets on $\mathbb N_{>0}$. Only the first sixteen labels are drawn; the dots continue indefinitely and are all included after the last excluded label. Spacing is for readability and defines no <metric>. Filled circles belong to the displayed <set>; crosses are excluded.}

The <cofinite topology> is useful for separating hypotheses that familiar <metric spaces> package together. An infinite cofinite <topological space> is a <T1 space>, because its <singletons> are <closed>, but is not a <Hausdorff space>, because two nonempty <open sets> cannot be <disjoint sets>. It is a <hyperconnected space> and hence a <connected space>. Moreover, <every subset of a cofinite space is compact>, and <sequence convergence in the cofinite topology> can have many limits. These are compatible properties, rather than contradictions: the usual uniqueness and closedness results use the <Hausdorff space> hypothesis.<Point-Set Topology by Romyar Sharifi/Chapter 1: Topological spaces>[Example 1.5.10 and Proposition 1.5.11: closed points, overlapping neighbourhoods, non-repeating sequences and uniqueness in Hausdorff spaces]{ref}

= Diagonal in a product with cofinite and Euclidean topologies
{parent=Cofinite topology}
{title2=$\{(t,t):t\in\mathbb R\}\subseteq\mathbb R_{\rm cof}\times\mathbb R\ \cong\ \mathbb R$}

Let $Z$ be the underlying <set> of <real numbers> with the <cofinite topology>. The diagonal $W=\{(t,t):t\in\mathbb R\}$ in $Z\times\mathbb R$ with its <subspace topology> is <homeomorphic> to the usual <real line>. Indeed, $t\mapsto(t,t)$ is a <continuous bijection>: its first coordinate is <continuous> because usual <finite sets> are <closed>, and its second coordinate is the identity. The inverse is the second <coordinate projection> restricted to $W$. Consequently $W$ is not <compact>, whereas $Z$ is <compact> by the fact that <every subset of a cofinite space is compact>. Thus $W$ is not <homeomorphic> to $Z$.

= Connected subsets of a cofinite space
{parent=Cofinite topology}

For a <set> $X$ with its <cofinite topology>, a <subset> $S$ is a <connected subset> exactly when $S$ is empty, a <singleton>, or infinite. Indeed, its <subspace topology> is the <cofinite topology> on $S$: the traces of <open sets> are precisely $S\setminus F$ for finite $F\subseteq S$, together with the <empty set>. For infinite $S$, two nonempty <open sets> intersect, because their <complements> have finite <union>. Hence $S$ is a <hyperconnected space> and therefore a <connected space>. A finite $S$ has the <discrete topology>, which admits a <separation of a topological space> as soon as $S$ has at least two points.

= Infinite disconnected subspace of a cofinite product
{parent=Connected subsets of a cofinite space}

For an infinite <set> $X$ with its <cofinite topology>, every infinite <subset> is <connected>, but $X\times X$ has an infinite disconnected <topological subspace>. Choose $a\ne b$ and use $D=\{a,b\}\times X$. The <open sets> $(X\setminus\{b\})\times X$ and $(X\setminus\{a\})\times X$ have disjoint nonempty traces $\{a\}\times X$ and $\{b\}\times X$ on $D$, covering it. Thus $D$ is a product of a discrete two-point subspace with an infinite cofinite factor. The ambient open rectangles may intersect; only their traces must be disjoint for a <separation> of $D$.

= Sequence convergence in the cofinite topology
{parent=Cofinite topology}

By <convergence of a sequence in a topological space>, $x_n\to a$ means that every <open> <neighbourhood> of $a$ contains all sufficiently late terms. In the <cofinite topology>, this is equivalent to the condition that, for every $b\ne a$, the value $b$ occurs only finitely often. Necessity uses the <open> <neighbourhood> $X\setminus\{b\}$; sufficiency chooses an index beyond every last occurrence of the finitely many points excluded by a given <open> <neighbourhood>.

Consequently a <sequence> with distinct terms converges to every point of $X$. For the original choice $x_n=n$ on $X=\mathbb N_{>0}$, take the two <open> <neighbourhoods>
$$
U_1=X\setminus\{2,5,8\}\ni1,\qquad U_2=X\setminus\{1,3,7\}\ni2.
$$
All terms with $n\geq9$ lie in $U_1$ and all terms with $n\geq8$ lie in $U_2$. More generally, each finite excluded <set> has a last visit, so $x_n\to1$ and $x_n\to2$, and in fact $x_n\to a$ for every $a\in X$. No numerical distance is tending to zero here; the <open sets> specify the meaning of convergence.

\Image[cofinite-sequence-multiple-neighbourhood-limits.png]
{title=The same <sequence> eventually stays in neighbourhoods of two different limits}
{description=Original sequence $x_n=n$ in the <cofinite topology>. Two sample <neighbourhoods> have different finite exclusions and different last-visit indices. Their memberships illustrate the general finite-exclusion argument; testing just these two neighbourhoods would not by itself prove convergence.}

Distinct terms are sufficient, not necessary. The <sequence> $1,1,2,2,3,3,\ldots$ also converges to every point, since each value occurs only twice. By contrast, the <constant sequence> $x_n=1$ converges only to $1$: an <open> <neighbourhood> of any $a\ne1$ can exclude $1$. The alternating <sequence> $1,2,1,2,\ldots$ converges to no point, since some value different from any proposed limit occurs infinitely often.

= Every subset of a cofinite space is compact
{parent=Cofinite topology}

Every <subset> $A$ of a <cofinite> <topological space> is a <compact subset>. To see the finite reduction directly, take an <open cover> of a nonempty $A$ by <open sets> of $X$ and choose one member $U$ containing a point of $A$. Since $U$ has finite <complement>, the <set> $A\setminus U$ is finite. Choose a covering member for each of those remaining points. Those members together with $U$ are a <finite subcover>. The <empty set> is already <compact>.

Equivalently, the <subspace topology> on $A$ is itself cofinite: for finite $F\subseteq X$, the trace of $X\setminus F$ is $A\setminus(F\cap A)$. The argument applies again inside $A$. A concrete <open cover> $\{X\setminus\{k\}:k\in X\}$ of $X=\mathbb N_{>0}$ needs only the two members $X\setminus\{1\}$ and $X\setminus\{2\}$ when these labels are distinct: each fills the other's single gap.

On $X=\mathbb N_{>0}$, the <subset> of <even integers> is <compact> but not <closed>, because it is infinite and proper. A <compact subset of a Hausdorff space> is guaranteed to be <closed>, whereas <compactness> without the <Hausdorff space> assumption does not suffice. Changing the <topology> changes <compactness>: the same infinite <set> with its <discrete topology> has the <singleton> <open cover>, which has no <finite subcover>. The finite-exception argument is special to finite <complements>; in the <cocountable topology>, <compact subsets of a cocountable space> must instead be finite.

= Continuous maps from an infinite cofinite space to a Hausdorff space are constant
{parent=Cofinite topology}

If $X$ is an <infinite set> with its <cofinite topology> and $Y$ is a <Hausdorff space>, every <continuous map> $f:X\to Y$ is a <constant function>. Otherwise two distinct image points would have <disjoint> <open> <neighbourhoods>. Their <inverse images> would be nonempty <disjoint> <open sets> of $X$, contradicting its <hyperconnected space> property.

For example, $f(n)=n$ from $\mathbb N_{>0}$ with its <cofinite topology> into the <real line> with its usual <Euclidean metric> is not <continuous>: the <inverse image> of the <open interval> $(1/2,3/2)$ is $\{1\}$, which is not <open> in the infinite domain. A <continuous> real-valued readout on that domain can only report one value. Finite cofinite domains are different: their <discrete topology> allows arbitrary <functions> into any <topological space>.

= Hausdorff factorization through a cofinite product factor
{parent=Continuous maps from an infinite cofinite space to a Hausdorff space are constant}
{c}
{title2=$F:Z\times T\to Y\text{ continuous}\ \Longrightarrow\ F=g\circ\pi_T$}

Let $Z$ be a nonempty infinite <set> with the <cofinite topology>, let $T$ be any <topological space>, and let $Y$ be <Hausdorff>. For a <continuous map> $F:Z\times T\to Y$, every slice $z\mapsto F(z,t)$ is constant. Thus $F(z,t)=g(t)$, where $g(t)=F(z_0,t)$ for any fixed $z_0$. The map $g$ is <continuous>, since $t\mapsto(z_0,t)$ is <continuous> into the <product topology>. It is unique because the <coordinate projection> $\pi_T$ is a <surjection>. If $F$ is a <surjection>, so is $g$.

For $T=[0,1]$ and $Y$ <homeomorphic> to the <circle>, a surjective $g$ cannot be injective: otherwise the <compact-to-Hausdorff continuous bijection theorem> would contradict the fact that the <circle and interval are not homeomorphic>. Hence distinct $a,b\in[0,1]$ satisfy $F(z,a)=F(z,b)$ simultaneously for all $z\in Z$.

= Continuous maps from products of cofinite spaces to Hausdorff spaces are constant
{parent=Continuous maps from an infinite cofinite space to a Hausdorff space are constant}

A <continuous map> from a nonempty finite <product space> of infinite <sets> with their <cofinite topologies> to a <Hausdorff space> is a <constant function>. Fixing all but one coordinate gives a <continuous map> from an infinite <cofinite topology>, so changing that coordinate does not change the value. Any two points in a finite <Cartesian product> can be joined by finitely many one-coordinate changes. This proves the assertion. In particular every real-valued <continuous function> on $Z\times Z$ is constant when $Z$ is the <real line> equipped with the <cofinite topology>.

= Hahn-Mazurkiewicz theorem
{c}
{parent=Topology}
{wiki}

A nonempty Hausdorff space is the continuous image of $[0,1]$ exactly when it is compact, connected, locally connected, and second-countable.

= Peano continuum
{c}
{parent=Hahn-Mazurkiewicz theorem}
{wiki}

A Peano continuum is a compact, connected, locally connected metric space. By the <Hahn-Mazurkiewicz theorem>, every Peano continuum is the image of a continuous surjection from $[0,1]$.

\Include[topological-manifold]{parent=Topology}

= Closed set
{parent=Topology}
{wiki}
{title2=$X\setminus F\in\tau$}

= Closed
{synonym}

= Closed subset
{synonym}

A <subset> $F$ of a <topological space> $(X,\tau)$ is <closed> when its <complement> in that same ambient space is <open>: $X\setminus F\in\tau$. For example, the <closed interval> $[0,1]$ is <closed> in the usual <real line> because its <complement> is the <open set> $(-\infty,0)\cup(1,\infty)$. Always specify the ambient <topological space>: a <subset> can be <closed> in a <subspace topology> without being <closed> in the surrounding space. https://www.maths.ox.ac.uk/system/files/attachments/Preliminary%20Reading%20%281%29.pdf[Richard Haydon, Oxford topology preliminaries, Section 0.1]{ref}

Being <closed> is not the logical negation of being <open>. In the usual <real line>, all four combinations occur: $(0,1)$ is <open> but not <closed>; $[0,1]$ is <closed> but not <open>; the half-open <interval> $[0,1)$ is neither <open> nor <closed>; and both the <empty set> and the whole <real line> are <open> and <closed>, hence <clopen>. Nor does being <closed> imply being a <bounded set>: $[0,\infty)$ is <closed> because its <complement> $(-\infty,0)$ is <open>, but it is not a <bounded set>. The symbol $\infty$ is not an extra point that this <interval> must contain.

\Image[open-and-closed-sets-on-line.png]
{title=The four combinations of openness and closedness in the real line}

The coloured strokes mark membership; filled endpoint circles mean included points, and hollow endpoint circles mean excluded points. The <empty set> has no coloured points, while arrows at both ends of the coloured <real line> indicate that membership continues indefinitely. These examples use the usual <topology> of the <real line>.

In a <metric space> $X$, a <subset> $F$ is <closed> exactly when every <convergent sequence> $(x_n)$ in $F$ whose <limit of a sequence> $x$ lies in $X$ has $x\in F$. For example, $x_n=1-1/n$ lies in $[0,1)$ for $n\geq1$ and has <limit of a sequence> $1$, which is missing; thus this half-open <interval> is not <closed> in the <real line>. This criterion is useful for keeping limiting constructions inside a prescribed <set>. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-100b-real-analysis-spring-2025/mit18_100b_s25_lec_full.pdf[MIT Real Analysis, Lecture 13, closed sets and convergent sequences]{ref}

In every <topological space>, the general criterion is $F=\overline F$, using <closure>; equivalently, $F$ contains all its <limit points>. A <closed set> is always a <sequentially closed set>, but the converse can fail outside <metric spaces>. Arbitrary <intersections> and finite <unions> of <closed sets> are <closed>, by taking <complements> in the <topology axioms>.

= Closedness
{parent=Closed set}

The property of being a <closed subset> in a specified ambient <topological space>. In a <metric space>, a subset has this property exactly when it contains the limits of all its convergent sequences whose limits lie in the ambient space.

= Sequentially closed set
{parent=Closed set}

A <sequentially closed set> is one that contains every limit of its convergent sequences. This is weaker than being a <closed set> in a general <topological space>, but the two conditions agree in <metric spaces>.

= Limit point
{parent=Closed set}
{wiki}

= Accumulation point
{synonym}

A <limit point> of a <subset> $A$ of a <topological space> is a point $x$ whose every <neighbourhood> meets $A\setminus\{x\}$. Thus $1$ is a <limit point> of $[0,1)$ in the <real line>, even though $1\notin[0,1)$. In contrast, an <isolated point> of $A$ belongs to its <closure> but is not a <limit point> of $A$. This distinction separates <limit points> from the more inclusive notion of an <adherent point>.

= Three-stage accumulation tower
{parent=Limit point}

A subset of the plane can have three successive strict changes under taking its set of <accumulation points>. For example let
$$
A=\left\{\left(\frac1n+\frac1{n^2m},\frac1{n^2m^2k}\right):n,m,k\geq1\right\}.
$$
Put $B=\{(1/n+1/(n^2m),0):n,m\geq1\}$ and $C=\{(1/n,0):n\geq1\}$. Then $A'=B\cup C\cup\{0\}$, $A''=C\cup\{0\}$ and $A^{\prime\prime\prime}=\{0\}$, where $0$ denotes the origin. For fixed $n,m$, increasing $k$ gives a point of $B$; increasing $m$ gives a point of $C$; increasing $n$ gives the origin. These are all possible limits: in any <convergent sequence> of distinct points, either $n$ is unbounded, or a subsequence fixes $n$ and has unbounded $m$, or a subsequence fixes both and has unbounded $k$. The first-coordinate cluster <intervals> $[1/n,1/n+1/n^2]$ are disjoint and separated away from the origin. Repeating this classification after each differentiation proves the displayed derived sets. Each <derivative> removes one layer of <isolated points>, and all three displayed changes are strict.

= Closure
{title2=$\overline A$}
{parent=Topology}
{disambiguate=topology}
{wiki}

= Closure
{synonym}

The <closure> of a <subset> $A$ of a <topological space> $X$ is the smallest <closed set> containing $A$. Equivalently, it is the <intersection> of all <closed sets> containing $A$, or the <set> of points whose every <neighbourhood> in $X$ meets $A$. The notation $\operatorname{cl}_X A$ makes the ambient <topological space> explicit. This definition works without a <metric> or any appeal to <sequences>. https://math.mit.edu/~hrm/palestine/munkres-topology.pdf[James Munkres, Topology, closure and interior of a set]{ref}

For $A=[0,1)$ in the usual <real line>, $\overline A=[0,1]$: every <neighbourhood> of $1$ meets $A$, while a point outside $[0,1]$ has an <open ball> disjoint from $A$. Passing to the <closure> adds the missing endpoint and retains all original points, including <isolated points> when present. In general, the <closure> is $A$ together with its <limit points>; its individual points are the <adherent points> of $A$.

The <closure> turns an arbitrary <subset> into a <closed set> with no unnecessary added points. It satisfies $A\subseteq\overline A$ and $\overline{\overline A}=\overline A$; a <subset> is <closed> exactly when $A=\overline A$. In a <metric space>, $x\in\overline A$ exactly when some <sequence> of points of $A$ has <limit of a sequence> $x$: choose its $n$th point in $A\cap B(x,1/n)$. For a general <topological space>, keep the <neighbourhood> definition; <sequences> need not detect every point of the <closure>.

= Adherent point
{parent=Closure (topology)}
{title2=$x\in\overline A$}
{wiki}

An adherent point of $A$ is a point whose every <neighbourhood> meets $A$. The set of adherent points is the <closure> of $A$. This includes all points of $A$, even isolated ones; a <limit point> in the accumulation-point convention instead requires each neighbourhood to meet $A\setminus\{x\}$.

= Interior
{disambiguate=topology}
{parent=Topology}
{wiki}
{title2=$A^\circ=\operatorname{int}_X A$}

= Interior
{synonym}

= Interior of a set
{synonym}

The <interior> of a <subset> $A$ of a <topological space> $X$ is the <union> of all <open sets> contained in $A$, equivalently the largest <open subset> of $A$. A point lies in $A^\circ=\operatorname{int}_X A$ exactly when it has an <open> <neighbourhood> contained in $A$. In a <metric space>, this says that some <open ball> $B(x,r)$, with $r>0$, is contained in $A$. The <interior> therefore identifies the points with room to move locally while staying in $A$. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-100a-real-analysis-fall-2020/mit18_100af20_basic_analysis.pdf[Jiří Lebl, Basic Analysis, Definition 7.2.23]{ref}

For the half-open <interval> $A=[0,1)$ in the <real line>, $A^\circ=(0,1)$. Every point strictly between the endpoints has a sufficiently small <open ball> inside $A$, but every <open ball> about zero also contains negative points. Although $0\in A$, it is not in the <interior>; it is a <boundary> point instead. Together, the <interior> and <closure> bracket the original <subset>: $A^\circ\subseteq A\subseteq\overline A$.

The ambient <topological space> matters: for $X=[0,1]$ with its <subspace topology>, $\operatorname{int}_X X=X$, whereas $\operatorname{int}_{\mathbb R}X=(0,1)$. The diagram accompanying <boundary of a set> illustrates the relative <open ball> at zero. A <subset> is <open> exactly when $A=A^\circ$, and taking the <interior> twice has the same effect as taking it once. The <complement> identity $\operatorname{int}_X A=X\setminus\operatorname{cl}_X(X\setminus A)$ links <interior> directly to <closure>.

= Topological space
{parent=Topology}
{wiki}
{title2=$(X,\tau)$}

A topological space is a <set> $X$ together with a <topology> $\tau\subseteq\mathcal P(X)$, a family of <subsets> satisfying
$$
\varnothing,X\in\tau,\qquad
\bigcup_{i\in I}U_i\in\tau\quad(U_i\in\tau),\qquad
\bigcap_{j=1}^nU_j\in\tau\quad(U_j\in\tau).
$$
Here $\mathcal P(X)$ is the <power set>; the <union> may have arbitrarily many members, while the <intersection> must be finite. The members of $\tau$ are the <open sets>. These are axioms about <subsets>, with no assumed <metric> or surrounding <Euclidean space>. https://www.maths.ox.ac.uk/system/files/attachments/Preliminary%20Reading%20%281%29.pdf[Richard Haydon, Oxford topology preliminaries, Definition 0.1]{ref}

\Image[finite-topology-examples.png]
{title=Two topologies on the same two-point set and continuity of the identity map}

For $X=\{a,b\}$, the <discrete topology> is $\tau_d=\{\varnothing,\{a\},\{b\},X\}$, while the <indiscrete topology> is $\tau_i=\{\varnothing,X\}$. Both satisfy the axioms. They describe different <topological spaces> on the same <set>: the first is <Hausdorff> and disconnected, while the second is <connected> and not <Hausdorff>. Each is <compact> because $X$ is a <finite set>.

The <identity function> $i:(X,\tau_d)\to(X,\tau_i)$ is a <continuous map>: the only target <open sets> have <inverse images> $\varnothing$ and $X$. Its inverse is not a <continuous map>, since the target <open set> $\{a\}$ has <inverse image> $\{a\}$, which is not an <open set> in $\tau_i$. Thus even a <continuous> <bijection> need not be a <homeomorphism>.

= Lindelöf space
{parent=Topological space}
{c}

= Lindelöf
{synonym}

A <Lindelöf space> is a <topological space> in which every <open cover> has a <countable> subcover. A <closed discrete subset> of a <Lindelöf space> must be <countable>: cover each of its points by an <open set> meeting it at only that point and add the <open> <complement>. In a <metrizable space>, the <Lindelöf> property is equivalent to <topological separability>.

= Fortissimo space
{parent=Topological space}

Choose an uncountable <set> $X$ and a point $a\in X$. Every point other than $a$ is isolated, and a set containing $a$ is <open> precisely when its complement is countable. Equivalently, the <closed sets> are all subsets containing $a$ together with all countable subsets. Arbitrary intersections and finite unions preserve this latter condition, so it defines a <topology>.

Every convergent <sequence> in this space is eventually equal to its limit. Indeed the set of its values different from its limit is countable and <closed>; its complement is an <open neighbourhood> of the limit. Eventual membership in this complement forces eventual equality. Nevertheless $a$ is not isolated, because its singleton has uncountable complement. In particular <convergence of a sequence in a topological space>[sequence convergence] alone does not determine this topology.

A real-valued <function> on this space is <continuous> exactly when $\{x:f(x)\ne f(a)\}$ is countable. Sufficiency follows by considering the <preimages> of closed subsets of the <real line>: each either contains $a$ or is a subset of that countable exceptional set. For necessity, each set $\{x:|f(x)-f(a)|\geq1/n\}$ is closed and excludes $a$, hence is countable; their countable union is the exceptional set.

= First-countable space
{parent=Topological space}
{wiki}

= First countable
{synonym}

= First countability
{synonym}

A <topological space> is first-countable when each point has a countable <neighbourhood basis>. Every <metric space> is first-countable: the <open balls> $B(x,1/n)$ form such a basis. A subset of a first-countable space remains first-countable in its <subspace topology>, since intersecting a local basis with the subset preserves the defining containment property. Thus a space failing first countability cannot be <homeomorphic> to any <subset> of a <metric space>.

= Warsaw circle
{parent=Topological space}
{c}

The <Warsaw circle> joins the two <path components> of a closed <topologist's sine curve> by an <arc> attached at their outer endpoints. It is <path-connected> and has trivial <fundamental group>, yet admits a <continuous map> to the <circle> with no <continuous lift> to its real-line <covering space>. Set the map equal to $1$ on the closed <topologist's sine curve> and wind once on the joining <arc>. A lift would be constant on each <path component> of the sine curve, with the same constant on both by <continuity>, but would change by $1$ along the joining <arc>. This proves that the map is not <null-homotopic>, by the <homotopy lifting theorem for a covering map>. Thus finiteness of the <fundamental group> alone cannot replace <local path connectedness> in the usual <lifting criterion for a covering space>.

= Convergence of a sequence in a topological space
{parent=Topological space}
{title2=$x_n\to x$}

A <sequence> $(x_n)$ in a <topological space> converges to $x$ if every <open> <neighbourhood> of $x$ contains all sufficiently late terms:
$$
\forall U\text{ open with }x\in U\quad\exists N\quad\forall n\geq N:\ x_n\in U.
$$
For a <metric space>, this is the usual <limit of a sequence> condition expressed through <open balls>. For a <Hausdorff space>, distinct proposed limits have <disjoint> <open> <neighbourhoods>, so a <sequence> cannot eventually stay in both and the limit is unique. The <sequence convergence in the cofinite topology> demonstrates what can change when that separation property is absent.<Point-Set Topology by Romyar Sharifi/Chapter 1: Topological spaces>[Definition 1.5.5, Proposition 1.5.11 and Proposition 1.6.20: neighbourhood convergence, uniqueness and the metric formulation]{ref}

= Open and closed maps
{parent=Topological space}
{wiki}

An <open map> sends each <open set> to an <open set>; a closed map sends each <closed set> to a <closed set>. These properties concern images, whereas <continuity> concerns inverse images of open sets. Neither openness nor closedness alone implies continuity.

= Stratified space
{parent=Topological space}

A space partitioned into <manifold> strata, with a locally finite frontier-compatible partition. In the conical setting, a neighbourhood of a point in an $s$-dimensional stratum has the form $\mathbb R^s\times cL$, respecting the strata, for a compact stratified link $L$.

= Stratum of a stratified space
{parent=Stratified space}

= Strata of a stratified space
{synonym}

A stratum is one <manifold> piece of a stratification, with locally constant dimension and prescribed conical normal neighbourhoods. Its codimension in a pure $N$-dimensional space is $N$ minus its dimension.

= Stratified pseudomanifold
{parent=Stratified space}

= Pseudomanifold
{synonym}

An $N$-dimensional conically <stratified space> has a closed filtration $\varnothing=X_{-1}\subseteq X_0\subseteq\cdots\subseteq X_N=X$, with each nonempty $X_j\setminus X_{j-1}$ a $j$-manifold, $X_{N-1}=X_{N-2}$, and dense top stratum. At an $s$-stratum the conical link is a compact $(N-s-1)$-dimensional <stratified pseudomanifold>. In dimension zero a compact link is a finite set. Connected links are an additional normality condition, not part of this definition.

= Normalization of a two-dimensional pseudomanifold
{parent=Stratified pseudomanifold}

The link of an isolated stratum of a closed two-dimensional pseudomanifold is a finite disjoint union of circles. Separate these branches, replacing the cone on their union by separate disks. This produces a closed <surface> and a finite surjective map to the original space, a <homeomorphism> on the regular stratum. <Orientations> on the regular stratum extend over the disks.

= Witt space
{parent=Stratified pseudomanifold}
{c}

A rational <Witt space> is a <stratified pseudomanifold> such that the link $L$ of every stratum of odd codimension $2r+1$ satisfies $IH_r^{\bar m}(L;\mathbb Q)=0$. This removes the only difference between lower and upper middle extensions and gives rational middle-perversity <Poincare duality> on closed oriented <Witt spaces>.

= Witt space with collared boundary
{parent=Witt space}
{c}

A compact <stratified space> with boundary is a <Witt space> with collared boundary when its interior and boundary are <Witt spaces> and a stratified neighbourhood of the boundary is $\partial X\times[0,\varepsilon)$, with product stratification. <Orientations> are carried by the regular strata and induce the usual boundary <orientation>.

= Link of a stratum
{parent=Stratified pseudomanifold}

= Links of a stratum
{synonym}

The compact space $L$ in a stratified neighbourhood $\mathbb R^s\times cL$ is the link of the stratum. Its dimension is one less than the codimension of that stratum. A disconnected link records distinct local branches.

= Continuous real maps from the initial-segment topology
{parent=Topological space}

Give $\mathbb N$ the <topology> whose nonempty <open sets> are $\mathbb N$ and the finite initial segments. Every nonempty <open set> contains $1$, so any two nonempty <open sets> intersect. A <continuous map> from this <topological space> into a <Hausdorff space> is constant: distinct image points would have disjoint <open> <neighbourhoods>, whose inverse images would be disjoint nonempty <open sets>. This includes <continuous> real-valued maps.

= Sigma-compact space
{parent=Topological space}
{title2=$X=\bigcup_{n\geq1}K_n,\quad K_n\text{ compact}$}

A space is sigma-compact if it is a countable union of compact subsets. A locally compact, sigma-compact <metrizable space> is <second-countable>: each compact metrizable subset has a countable dense set, their union is countable and dense, and a separable metric space has a countable basis.

= Sober space
{parent=Topological space}
{wiki}

= Sober topological space
{synonym}

A <topological space> is sober when every nonempty <irreducible closed subset> is the closure of a unique point. Every <Hausdorff space> is sober. In contrast, an infinite set with the <cofinite topology> has the whole space irreducible and closed, while every singleton is closed, so it is not sober.

= Sobrification
{parent=Sober space}
{title2=$\eta:X\to\operatorname{Sob}(X),\quad x\mapsto\overline{\{x\}}$}

The sobrification has one point for every nonempty <irreducible closed subset> $A$ of $X$, with opens $\widehat U=\{A:A\cap U\ne\varnothing\}$. Irreducibility gives $\widehat U\cap\widehat V=\widehat{U\cap V}$, and the map from original opens to these opens is bijective, detected by singleton closures. An irreducible closed subset of the new space has complement $\widehat W$; $F=X\setminus W$ is an irreducible closed subset of $X$ and uniquely generates it. Thus the new space is a <sober space>.

= Separable topological space
{parent=Topological space}

= Topological separability
{synonym}

= Separable
{synonym}

A <topological space> is separable when it has a countable <dense subset>. The topology is part of the assertion: a dual may be separable in its <weak-star topology> and not in its <norm topology>. A compact <metric space> is separable by taking the union of finite nets at scales tending to zero.

= G-delta set
{c}
{parent=Topological space}
{title2=$G_\delta=\bigcap_{n\ge1}U_n\quad(U_n\text{ open})$}

A G-delta set is a countable intersection of open subsets of a topological space. In a complete <metric> space these are precisely the <topologically complete> subspaces, by the <G-delta criterion for topological completeness>.

= Net
{parent=Topological space}
{title2=$(x_\alpha)_{\alpha\in D}$}
{disambiguate=mathematics}
{wiki}

= Net
{synonym}

A net is a family indexed by a directed ordered set: every pair of indices has a common upper bound. It converges to $x$ when it is eventually in every neighbourhood of $x$. Unlike <sequences>, nets characterize closure in every <topological space>: a point is in the closure of a set precisely when some net in that set converges to it.

= Subnet of a net
{parent=Net (mathematics)}

= Subnet
{synonym}

A subnet is obtained from a <net> by an order-preserving cofinal map from another directed index set. It preserves any limit of the original net, and eventual properties still hold along it. Every net in a <compact set> has a convergent subnet. This supplies compactness arguments when sequential compactness is unavailable.

= Separation axiom
{parent=Topological space}
{wiki}

A separation axiom is a condition on a <topological space> expressing how points or closed subsets can be distinguished by <open sets>. The <Kolmogorov space> condition distinguishes distinct points by some open set; the <T1 space> condition makes every point closed; the stronger <Hausdorff space> condition gives disjoint neighbourhoods to distinct points.

= Regular topological space
{parent=Separation axiom}

= Regularity of a topological space
{synonym}

A <topological space> is regular when a <point> and a <closed set> not containing it have disjoint <open neighborhoods>. Equivalently, for every <point> $x$ in an <open set> $U$, there is an open $W$ with $x\in W\subseteq\overline W\subseteq U$. To obtain shrinking, separate $x$ from the closed complement of $U$; the <closure> of the <point>'s <neighborhood> avoids the other <neighborhood>. Conversely use such a shrinking and the complement of its <closure>. This definition alone does not require $T_1$.

= T3 space
{parent=Regular topological space}
{c}
{title2=$T_3$}

A <T3 space> is a <regular topological space> which is also a <T1 space>. Every <metric space> has this property: singletons are closed, and a sufficiently small ball about a <point> outside a <closed set> has <closure> avoiding that set. A second-<countable> <T3 space> is a <metrizable space> by <Urysohn's metrization theorem>.

= Totally separated space
{parent=Separation axiom}

A <topological space> is totally separated when any two distinct <points> are separated by a <clopen set>. Such a space has no nontrivial <connected subset>. A <zero-dimensional space> with its usual <clopen>-base definition and $T_1$ is totally separated. Conversely these conditions agree for <compact Hausdorff spaces> by the <Šura-Bura lemma> and <compactness>, although the definitions should not be conflated for arbitrary spaces. Any closed totally separated subset of the unit square is <nowhere dense>, since every nonempty relatively open subset of the square contains a nontrivial <connected> <line segment>. Restricting a <clopen set> separating the segment's endpoints would disconnect that segment.

= T1 space
{parent=Separation axiom}
{c}
{title2=$T_1$}

A <topological space> is T1 when every point is a <closed point>. Equivalently, for distinct $x,y$ there is an open set containing $x$ but not $y$, and an open set containing $y$ but not $x$. These open sets need not be disjoint. Every <Hausdorff space> is T1, but an infinite set with its <cofinite topology> is T1 without being Hausdorff.

= Closed point
{parent=Topological space}
{title2=$\{x\}\text{ is closed}$}

A point of a <topological space> is closed when its singleton is a <closed set>. In a <T1 space> every point is closed. The closed points of an <affine scheme> $\operatorname{Spec}A$ are its <maximal ideals>; the closure of the point corresponding to $\mathfrak p$ is $V(\mathfrak p)$.

= Locally connected space
{parent=Topological space}
{wiki}

= Local connectedness
{synonym}

A <topological space> has <local connectedness> when every point has a <neighborhood> basis of <connected> <open sets>. In particular, a planar <domain> has <local connectedness>, but its <domain boundary> need not be. <Local connectedness> of the <boundary> is the condition governing a continuous extension of a <conformal bijection> to the parametrizing circle.

= Boundary of a set
{parent=Topological space}
{title2=$\partial A=\overline A\setminus A^\circ$}

= Boundary
{synonym}

The <boundary> of a <subset> $A$ of a <topological space> $X$ consists of the points whose every <neighbourhood> in $X$ meets both $A$ and its <complement> $X\setminus A$. Equivalently,
$$
\partial_X A=\operatorname{cl}_X A\setminus\operatorname{int}_X A
=\operatorname{cl}_X A\cap\operatorname{cl}_X(X\setminus A).
$$
Here the two operations are <closure> and <interior>. The second formula shows that the <boundary> is a <closed set> and that $A$ and its <complement> have the same <boundary>. It locates precisely the points where membership cannot be decided from a small <neighbourhood> alone. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-100a-real-analysis-fall-2020/mit18_100af20_basic_analysis.pdf[Jiří Lebl, Basic Analysis, Definition 7.2.23 and Propositions 7.2.26–7.2.27]{ref}

For $A=[0,1)$ in the usual <real line>, the <interior> is $(0,1)$, the <closure> is $[0,1]$, and the <boundary> is $\{0,1\}$. In particular, $0$ belongs both to $A$ and to its <boundary>, while $1$ belongs to the <boundary> but not to $A$. A <boundary> point need not be excluded from the original <set>: <open sets> exclude all their <boundary> points, <closed sets> contain all theirs, and this half-open <interval> does one of each.

\Image[relative-openness-and-boundary.png]
{title=Relative openness in a closed interval and the boundary of a half-open interval}

The upper panel visualizes the relative example from <open set>: for $X=[0,1]$ and $A=[0,1/2)$, an <open ball> about zero in $X$ is $B_X(0,r)=X\cap(-r,r)=[0,r)$ when $0<r<1/2$. This <open ball> stays in $A$; points outside $X$ are not required. The middle panel shows that $X$ is <clopen> in itself, with <interior> and <closure> both $X$ and empty <boundary>, although its <boundary> in the <real line> is $\{0,1\}$. The lower panel returns to ambient space $\mathbb R$ and separates the <interior>, <closure>, and <boundary> of $[0,1)$; both marked <boundary> points are included in the <boundary> itself.

= Boundary point
{parent=Boundary of a set}

A point belonging to the <boundary> of a set: every open neighborhood meets both the set and its complement.

= Boundary component
{parent=Boundary of a set}

A <connected component> of a <boundary>. A <surface> with a hole can have several <boundary components>, each with its own induced <boundary orientation>.

= Boundary of a domain
{parent=Boundary of a set}
{title2=$\partial D$}

= Domain boundary
{synonym}

The <boundary> of a planar <domain> $D$ is its <boundary of a set>. It is distinct from the <boundary> of a parametrizing interval: a curve can begin on $\partial D$ while all its positive-time points lie inside $D$.

= Basis of a topology
{parent=Topological space}

= Open basis
{synonym}

= Topological basis
{synonym}

A basis of a topology is a family of open sets such that every open set is a union of members of that family. Equivalently, every point of every open set lies in a basis member contained in it. The rectangles $U\times V$ form a basis for the <product topology> of two spaces. This topological meaning differs from a linear-algebra <basis>.

= Subbasis of a topology
{parent=Basis of a topology}
{title2=$\mathcal S$}

A family of open subsets is a <subbasis of a topology> when its finite intersections form a <basis of a topology>, including the empty <intersection> equal to the entire space. For the <product topology>, <inverse images> of <open sets> under the <product projections> form the standard <subbasis of a topology>. A map into the space is continuous if the <inverse image> of every subbasic set is open.

= Second-countable space
{parent=Topological space}

= Second countability
{synonym}

= Second countable
{synonym}

A second-countable <topological space> has a countable collection of open sets such that every open set is a union of members of that collection. A space covered by countably many open <manifold charts> is second countable because each coordinate domain has a countable basis of rational balls.

= Paracompact space
{parent=Topological space}
{wiki}

= Paracompact
{synonym}

A topological space is paracompact when every <open cover> has a locally finite open refinement. Every <metric space> and every second-countable <topological manifold> is paracompact.

= Kolmogorov space
{title2=$T_0$}
{parent=Topological space}
{wiki}
{c}

= T0 space
{synonym}

A topological space is $T_0$ when every pair of distinct points is distinguished by an <open set> containing exactly one of them.

= Sierpiński space
{title2=$S$}
{c}
{parent=Kolmogorov space}
{wiki}

The Sierpiński space is $\{0,1\}$ with open sets $\varnothing$, $\{1\}$, and $\{0,1\}$. Continuous maps $X\to S$ are in bijection with open subsets of $X$ through $g\mapsto g^{-1}(1)$.

= Embedding of a T0 space into a power of the Sierpiński space
{title2=$X\hookrightarrow S^{\mathbf{Top}(X,S)}$}
{c}
{parent=Sierpiński space}

For a <T0 space> $X$, evaluation against all continuous maps $g:X\to S$ is a <homeomorphism> onto its image under the map
$$
x\longmapsto(g(x))_g
$$
into a power of the <Sierpiński space>. The $T_0$ axiom makes it injective, and the coordinate inverse images of $\{1\}$ recover every open subset of $X$.

= Continuous map
{parent=Topological space}
{title2=$f:X\to Y$}

A map $f:X\to Y$ between <topological spaces> is continuous when every <open set> $V\subseteq Y$ has an <inverse image> $f^{-1}(V)=\{x\in X:f(x)\in V\}$ that is an <open set> in $X$. The notation denotes a <subset>, so $f$ need not have an inverse <function>.

For the <continuous map> $f:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R$, $f(x)=x^2$, with the usual <metric topology>,
$$
f^{-1}((1,4))=(-2,-1)\cup(1,2).
$$
The two <open intervals> form an <open set>; an <inverse image> can split into separate pieces without violating <continuity>. The defining condition concerns <inverse images>, rather than images: here $f((-1,1))=[0,1)$ is not an <open set> in the <real line>. Checking this one <inverse image> illustrates the definition; it alone does not prove <continuity> for all target <open sets>.

= Boundedness extends across closure under a continuous map
{parent=Continuous map}
{title2=$f(A)\text{ bounded}\Longrightarrow f(\overline A)\text{ bounded}$}

For a <continuous map> into a <metric space>, $f(\overline A)\subseteq\overline{f(A)}$: an <open neighborhood> of an image point pulls back to a <neighborhood> meeting $A$. A <bounded set> lies in a <closed ball>, and its <closure> lies in the same ball because <distance> from the centre is a <continuous function>. The displayed boundedness conclusion follows. Neither <compactness> of the domain nor uniform <continuity> of the map is needed.

= Separate continuity
{parent=Continuous map}

= Separately continuous
{synonym}

A <function> of several variables is <separately continuous> when it is <continuous> in each variable with the others fixed. This is weaker than joint <continuity> in the <product topology>. <Functions> with narrow rectangular supports provide useful examples distinguishing <convergence> in <topologies> generated by <separate continuity> from Euclidean <convergence>.

= Rectangular bumps detecting nonconvergence
{parent=Separate continuity}
{title2=$B(x,y)=\sup_n\phi((x-u_n)/r_n)\phi((y-v_n)/s_n)$}

Let $(u_n,v_n)\to(0,0)$ with both coordinates nonzero, $r_n=|u_n|/4$, $s_n=|v_n|/4$, and $\phi(t)=\max(1-|t|,0)$. The displayed <function> equals one at every $(u_n,v_n)$ and zero on both coordinate axes. Each coordinate line off the corresponding axis meets only finitely many supports, so its restriction is a <maximum>[finite maximum] of <continuous functions>. On the axes it is zero. Hence $B$ is <separately continuous> and disproves <convergence> of this <sequence> in any <topology> making every <separately continuous> <function> <continuous>. Isotropic radial bumps need not have this property when one coordinate approaches zero much faster than the other.

= Closure characterization of continuity
{parent=Continuous map}

A <function> $f:X\to Y$ between <topological spaces> is <continuous> if and only if $f(\overline A)\subseteq\overline{f(A)}$ for every <subset> $A\subseteq X$, where the bars denote <closure>. For a <continuous> $f$, pull back an <open neighbourhood> of $f(x)$ for $x\in\overline A$; its <preimage> meets $A$, so the neighbourhood meets $f(A)$. Conversely, apply the inclusion to $A=f^{-1}(C)$ with $C$ <closed>. Then $f(\overline A)\subseteq C$ implies $\overline A\subseteq A$, so the <preimage> of every <closed subset> is <closed> and $f$ is <continuous>.

= Closed-set criterion for continuity
{parent=Continuous map}
{title2=$f\text{ continuous}\iff f^{-1}(F)\text{ closed for every closed }F$}

A <function> $f:X\to Y$ between <topological spaces> is <continuous> exactly when the <inverse image> of every <closed set> in $Y$ is <closed> in $X$. The defining <open set> criterion is equivalent, because $f^{-1}(Y\setminus A)=X\setminus f^{-1}(A)$. This criterion concerns <inverse images>; a <continuous map> need not be a <closed map>.

= Continuous injection
{parent=Continuous map}
{title2=$f:X\hookrightarrow Y$}

A <continuous map> that is also an <injective function>. A <continuous injection> need not be a <topological embedding>: its inverse on its <image> need not be <continuous>. If its source is a <compact space> and its target is a <Hausdorff space>, however, the <compact-to-Hausdorff continuous bijection theorem> makes that inverse <continuous>.

= Continuous bijection
{parent=Continuous map}
{title2=$f\text{ continuous and bijective}$}

A continuous bijection is a <continuous map> that is a <bijection>. It need not be a <homeomorphism>, since its <inverse function> need not be <continuous>. The <compact-to-Hausdorff continuous bijection theorem> supplies a useful sufficient condition for the stronger conclusion.

= Continuous lift
{parent=Continuous map}
{title2=$p\widetilde f=f$}

For <continuous maps> $p:E\to B$ and $f:X\to B$, a <continuous lift> of $f$ through $p$ is a <continuous map> $\widetilde f:X\to E$ with $p\widetilde f=f$. Existence and uniqueness require additional hypotheses; the <lifting criterion for a covering space> states a useful existence criterion when $p$ is a <covering map> and $X$ is <path-connected> and <locally path-connected>.

= Scalar clipping
{parent=Continuous map}
{title2=$g(x)=\min(b,\max(a,x))$}

= Clipping of a scalar
{synonym}

The <continuous map> projecting a real number onto the <closed interval> $[a,b]$, with $a\le b$. It equals $a$ below the interval, equals the input inside it, and equals $b$ above it. It is nondecreasing and has <Lipschitz constant> one.

= Topological embedding
{parent=Continuous map}
{title2=$f:X\hookrightarrow Y$}

A <continuous map> <injective> map $f:X\to Y$ that is a <homeomorphism> onto its image with the <subspace topology>. A <continuous map> <injective> map from a <compact> space into a <Hausdorff space> is a <topological embedding>.

= Evaluation embedding of a normal Hausdorff space
{parent=Topological embedding}
{title2=$e(x)=(f(x))_{f\in C(X,[0,1])}$}

<Continuous functions> $X\to[0,1]$ on a normal <Hausdorff space> separate points and <closed sets> by <Urysohn's lemma>. The evaluation map into the product of their codomains is <injective> and <continuous>. For each point in an <open set>, a coordinate equal to one there and zero outside the <open set> detects a smaller coordinate <neighborhood>, proving the inverse <topology>.

= C-embedded subset
{c}
{parent=Topological embedding}
{title2=$C(Y)=C(X)|_Y$}

A <subset> $Y\subseteq X$ is <C-embedded subset>[C-embedded] if every <continuous> <real-valued function> on its <subspace topology> extends continuously to $X$. <Closed subsets> of <normal spaces> have this property by the <Tietze extension theorem>. The condition also makes sense for nonclosed <subsets>.

= Cb-embedded subset
{c}
{parent=C-embedded subset}
{title2=$C^b(Y)=C^b(X)|_Y$}

A <subset> is <Cb-embedded subset>[Cb-embedded] if every bounded <continuous> <real-valued function> on it extends to a bounded <continuous> <real-valued function> on the ambient space. A bounded extension can be clipped to preserve the original bound. This is weaker than <C-embedded subset>[C-embeddedness]: the <natural numbers> in their <Stone-Čech compactification> have the bounded extension property, but their unbounded identity <function> cannot extend to a <compact space>.

= Diagonal evaluation map
{parent=Topological embedding}
{title2=$\Delta(x)=(f_i(x))_{i\in I}$}

A family of maps with a common domain determines the displayed map into the <Cartesian product> of their codomains. It is injective exactly when the family separates points. For continuous coordinate maps it is continuous in the <product topology>. If inverse images of open sets under the coordinates generate the original topology, the inverse from the image is also continuous; thus the map is a <topological embedding>. A basis of such inverse images, as opposed to a subbasis, is a sufficient stronger hypothesis.

= Universal three-point space for product embeddings
{parent=Topological embedding}
{title2=$Y=(\{0,1,2\},\{\varnothing,\{2\},Y\})$}

Every <topological space>, including one without the <T0 space> property, embeds into a power of this three-point space. For each <open set> $U$, use a coordinate equal to $2$ on $U$ and $0$ elsewhere. Its <inverse image> of the only proper nonempty <open set> is $U$. For each point $a$, add a coordinate equal to $1$ at $a$ and $0$ elsewhere. These coordinates are continuous because the subspace $\{0,1\}$ has the <indiscrete topology>, and they separate all points. The combined diagonal map is <injective> and induces precisely the original <topology>, hence is a <topological embedding>.

= Retraction
{parent=Topological space}
{wiki}

A retraction of a <topological space> $X$ onto a subspace $A$ is a continuous map $r:X\to A$ whose restriction to $A$ is the identity.

= Retraction of a Hilbert ball onto its sphere
{parent=Retraction}

The <closed unit ball> $B$ in the infinite-dimensional real <Hilbert space> $\ell^2$ admits a <retraction> onto its <unit sphere>. Write $S$ for the forward <unilateral shift operator> and set $F(x)=\sqrt{1-\|x\|^2}\,e_0+Sx$. Then $\|F(x)\|=1$ and $F(x)\ne x$: equality would force all coordinates of $x$ to coincide, hence vanish, contradicting $F(0)=e_0$.

For $d=x-F(x)$, set
$$
r(x)=F(x)+\frac{2(1-\langle F(x),x\rangle)}{\|d\|^2}d.
$$
The nonvanishing denominator makes $r$ a <continuous map>. Expanding its squared <norm> gives $\|r(x)\|=1$. On $\|x\|=1$, the fraction is one, so $r(x)=x$. Thus the finite-dimensional <no-retraction theorem> does not extend to this <closed unit ball>.

= Radial retraction of a punctured ball
{parent=Retraction}
{title2=$r(x)=a+\tau(x)(x-a)$}

Removing any interior point $a$ from the Euclidean <closed unit ball> allows a <retraction> onto its <unit sphere>. For $v=x-a\ne0$, the positive root of $|a+tv|^2=1$ is
$$
\tau(x)=\frac{-a\cdot v+\sqrt{(a\cdot v)^2+(1-|a|^2)|v|^2}}{|v|^2}.
$$
Then $r(x)=a+\tau(x)v$ is a <continuous map> on the punctured <closed unit ball>, has <norm> one, and fixes its <boundary> because $\tau(x)=1$ there. The <quadratic equation> has one positive and one negative root since $|a|<1$, so the chosen root is unambiguous.

= Retraction onto a punctured oriented surface
{parent=Retraction}
{title2=$\Sigma_h^\partial\subset\Sigma_g\text{ is a retract}\iff2h\leq g$}

A once-punctured genus-$h$ <closed orientable surface> retracts to a wedge of $2h$ circles. A <retraction> from $\Sigma_g$ would inject its degree-one <cohomology> as an <isotropic subspace of a symplectic vector space> for the <Poincare duality pairing>, since the punctured surface has zero degree-two cohomology. The maximal isotropic dimension is $g$, giving $2h\leq g$. Equality is realized by folding the double of the punctured surface onto one copy. Additional handles in the other copy can be pinched away while fixing the joining boundary, proving sufficiency for every embedding of such a one-boundary subsurface when $g\geq2h$.

= Subspace topology
{parent=Topological space}
{wiki}

For $A\subseteq X$, the subspace topology consists of the intersections $A\cap U$ with open subsets $U\subseteq X$.

= Locally closed subset
{parent=Subspace topology}

A subset $B$ of a <topological space> $X$ is locally closed if $B=U\cap F$ for an <open set> $U$ and a <closed set> $F$. Equivalently, $B$ is open in its <closure> $\overline B$, or $\overline B\setminus B$ is closed in $X$. For the equivalence, $B\subseteq F$ implies $\overline B\subseteq F$, so $B=U\cap\overline B$. Relative complementation proves the second condition implies the third. Conversely, if $C=\overline B\setminus B$ is closed, then $B=(X\setminus C)\cap\overline B$ is such an intersection.

= Topological subspace
{parent=Subspace topology}
{title2=$(A,\{A\cap U:U\text{ open in }X\})$}

A topological subspace of a <topological space> $X$ is a <subset> $A\subseteq X$ equipped with the <subspace topology> inherited from $X$.

= Closed subspace
{parent=Topological subspace}

A closed subspace of a <topological space> $X$ is a <closed subset> of $X$ equipped with its <subspace topology>.

= Alexandroff extension
{title2=$X^+$}
{parent=Topological space}
{wiki}
{c}

= One-point compactification
{synonym}

The one-point compactification of a locally compact noncompact Hausdorff space adjoins one point whose neighbourhoods are complements of compact subsets. The result is compact Hausdorff.

= Proper maps extend to one-point compactifications
{parent=Alexandroff extension}
{title2=$\alpha f(\infty_X)=\infty_Y$}

For locally <compact> noncompact <Hausdorff spaces>, a <continuous> <proper map> $f:X\to Y$ extends continuously to their <one-point compactifications> by the displayed rule. The inverse image of $\alpha Y\setminus K$ is $\alpha X\setminus f^{-1}(K)$, a <neighborhood> of infinity because the inverse image of a <compact set> is <compact>. The extension itself is proper, since it is <continuous> from a <compact space> to a <Hausdorff space>. A nonproper map can fail to admit any <continuous> extension: projection $\mathbb R^2\to\mathbb R$ sends two vertical sequences escaping to the same <point> at infinity to two distinct constant sequences, incompatible with <continuity> at that <point>.

= Uncountable discrete one-point compactification counterexample
{parent=Alexandroff extension}

The <one-point compactification> $D\cup\{\infty\}$ of an uncountable <discrete space> is a <hereditarily normal space> but not a <perfectly normal space>. A subspace missing $\infty$ is discrete; one containing it is closed, hence compact Hausdorff and normal. However, every neighborhood of $\infty$ misses only finitely many points of $D$. A countable intersection misses at most countably many points, so the closed singleton $\{\infty\}$ is not a <G-delta set>.

= Hawaiian earring
{parent=Alexandroff extension}
{c}
{title2=$\mathbb H$}
{wiki}

The Hawaiian earring is the union of a countable family of circles tangent at one point, with their radii tending to zero, endowed with its planar subspace topology. Equivalently it is the <one-point compactification> of a countable disjoint union of real lines. Every neighbourhood of the common point contains all but finitely many whole circles. This compact <topological space> is not locally contractible there and has different <singular homology> from the infinite <CW complex> wedge of circles.

= Rational summand in Hawaiian earring homology
{parent=Hawaiian earring}
{title2=$\mathbb Q\text{ is a direct summand of }H_1(\mathbb H;\mathbb Z)$}

The first integral <singular homology> of the <Hawaiian earring> has a <direct summand> isomorphic to $\mathbb Q$. This consequence of its structural homology theorem suffices, through the <universal coefficient theorem for cohomology> and <nonzero Ext of the rationals with integer coefficients>, to show that its integral $H^2$ is nonzero. The precise structural theorem is Theorem 3.1 of https://www.sas.rochester.edu/mth/sites/doug-ravenel/otherpapers/eda-kawamura2.pdf.

= Circle
{title2=$S^1$}
{parent=Topological space}
{wiki}

The circle is the subspace $S^1=\{z\in\mathbb C:|z|=1\}$, equivalently the quotient of a closed interval obtained by identifying its endpoints.

= Injective self-map of a circle
{parent=Circle}

Every continuous injective map $g:S^1\to S^1$ is a <homeomorphism>. After normalizing $g(1)=1$ by a rotation, it is <homotopy>[homotopic] either to the identity or to complex conjugation, and has <degree of a continuous mapping>[degree] $1$ or $-1$.

To see this without assuming surjectivity, lift $t\mapsto g(e^{2\pi it})$ on $[0,1]$ through the <covering map> $u\mapsto e^{2\pi iu}$ to a path $G$ with $G(0)=0$. Injectivity makes $G$ injective on $[0,1)$, hence strictly monotone. Its endpoint $d=G(1)$ is an integer. Strict monotonicity excludes $d=0$; if $|d|>1$, the <intermediate value theorem> gives an interior integer value, contradicting injectivity of $g$. Thus $d=\pm1$, and $\exp(2\pi i((1-s)G(t)+sdt))$ descends to a <homotopy> from $g$ to $z\mapsto z^d$. If $g$ omitted a point, a continuous branch of the <complex argument> on the punctured circle would make it <null-homotopic>, contradicting this nonzero <degree of a continuous mapping>[degree]. Hence it is surjective. A continuous bijection from a <compact space> to a <Hausdorff space> is a <homeomorphism>.

= Classification of a real complex-circle equation
{parent=Circle}
{title2=$A|z|^2+\overline Bz+B\overline z+C=0$}

For real $A,C$ and complex $B$, if $A\ne0$, <completing the square> gives $|z+B/A|^2=(|B|^2-AC)/A^2$. A positive right side gives a <circle>; zero gives a single point; a negative value gives the <empty set>. If $A=0$ and $B\ne0$, the equation is the <straight line> $2\operatorname{Re}(\overline Bz)+C=0$. If $A=B=0$, it describes the whole <complex plane> when $C=0$, and the <empty set> otherwise. Thus unrestricted coefficients require both the single-point and whole-plane degeneracies.

= Sagitta
{parent=Circle}
{title2=$s=R-\sqrt{R^2-L^2/4}$}

The maximum distance between a circular arc and its chord of length $L$. For radius $R$ and $L\ll R$, expansion gives $s\simeq L^2/(8R)$. In a uniform transverse <magnetic field>, a charged track has $R=p/(|q|B)$, so the <sagitta> measures its <momentum>.

= Soddy circle
{parent=Circle}
{c}

= Inner Soddy circle
{parent=Soddy circle}

Given three mutually externally tangent <circles>, the inner Soddy circle lies in their curvilinear triangular gap and is tangent to all three. The <Descartes circle theorem> determines its radius from the three given radii.

= Power of a point
{parent=Circle}

The power of a point $P$ relative to a <circle> with center $O$ and <radius> $r$ is $OP^2-r^2$. If a line through $P$ meets the <circle> at $A$ and $B$, the product of the directed distances $PA$ and $PB$ equals this quantity.

= Tangent circles
{parent=Circle}

Two distinct <circles> are tangent when they meet at exactly one point. For external tangency the distance between centers is the sum of the <radii>; for internal tangency it is their absolute difference. These relations turn many <circle packing> problems into exact distance equations.

= Four contact points of a circle chain
{parent=Tangent circles}

Four positive-<radius> <circles> with pairwise disjoint interiors, successive <external tangencies>, and four distinct contact points have those contact points on a <circle>. Apply the reciprocal <Möbius transformation> $w=1/(z-T)$ at one contact $T$. The two <circles> through $T$ become disjoint <parallel lines>; the other two <disks> lie between them and avoid zero. <Collinearity for tangent circles between parallel lines> places the three finite transformed contacts on a <straight line>. That <straight line> misses zero: otherwise its segment across the strip would pass through zero, while that segment is contained in the two transformed closed <disks>. Its inverse is therefore a genuine <circle> through $T$, containing all four original contacts. Allowing <internal tangency> without the pictured disjoint-interior hypotheses admits collinear degeneracies instead.

= Collinearity for tangent circles between parallel lines
{parent=Tangent circles}

Let two positive-<radius> <circles> have <external tangency> at $Q$ and lie in the closed strip between two <parallel lines>, touching opposite boundaries at $P,R$. Then $P,Q,R$ are <collinear>. To prove this, choose coordinates with the boundaries $x=0,d$, put $P=(0,0)$ and $R=(d,h)$, and write the centres as $O_1=(r_1,0)$, $O_2=(d-r_2,h)$. The contact point is
$$
Q=\frac{r_2O_1+r_1O_2}{r_1+r_2}=\frac{r_1}{r_1+r_2}R.
$$
The closed <line segment> $PR$ is contained in the union of the two closed <disks>: its portions from $P$ to $Q$ and from $Q$ to $R$ are <chords>. The strip and <external tangency> hypotheses are essential to this particular formulation.

= Internal tangency
{parent=Tangent circles}
{title2=$|c_1-c_2|=|r_1-r_2|>0$}

Two distinct positive-<radius> <circles> have internal tangency when their boundaries meet at exactly one point and one <disk> contains the other. Their centre separation equals the absolute difference of their <radii>.

= External tangency
{parent=Tangent circles}
{title2=$|c_1-c_2|=r_1+r_2$}

Two distinct positive-<radius> <circles> have external tangency when their closed <disks> meet at exactly one point. The <Euclidean distance> between their centres equals the sum of their <radii>. The contact point divides the segment of centres internally in the ratio $r_1:r_2$.

= Tangent to a circle
{parent=Circle}

At a point $T$ on a <circle> with centre $O$, the <tangent line> is perpendicular to the <radius> $OT$. For example, differentiating $|\mathbf r(s)-O|^2=R^2$ along a <parametric curve> on the <circle> gives $(\mathbf r-O)\cdot\mathbf r'=0$, showing that the radial <vector> and tangent direction are <orthogonal vectors>. Conversely, the perpendicular <straight line> through $T$ has only that point in common with the <circle>: for a nonzero displacement $v$ along it, $|T+v-O|^2=R^2+|v|^2>R^2$.

= Circular segment
{parent=Circle}
{title2=$A=\tfrac12R^2(\phi-\sin\phi)$}

A <circular segment> is the part of a disk bounded by a chord and one of the two <circular arcs> joining its endpoints. For <radius> $R$ and the chosen arc's central <angle> $0\leq\phi\leq2\pi$ in <radians>, its <area> is $R^2(\phi-\sin\phi)/2$. Subtract the signed central <triangle> of area $R^2\sin\phi/2$ from the <circular sector> of area $R^2\phi/2$. Angles below $\pi$ give the smaller segment and angles above $\pi$ the larger one.

= Area enclosed by two chords and a circular arc
{parent=Circular segment}
{title2=$A=R^2(\theta+\sin\theta)$}

Take a <circle> of <radius> $R$, a vertex $A$ on its circumference, and two chords from $A$ symmetric about the diameter through $A$. If their <angle> is $0\leq\theta\leq\pi$, choose the arc between their other endpoints that does not contain $A$. The <area> bounded by the two chords and that arc is $R^2(\theta+\sin\theta)$. To calculate it, set $A=(0,R)$ and the other endpoints to $(\pm R\sin\theta,-R\cos\theta)$. Their <triangle> has area $R^2\sin\theta(1+\cos\theta)$, and the adjoining <circular segment>, of central <angle> $2\theta$, has area $R^2(\theta-\sin\theta\cos\theta)$. The sum gives the formula, including the limiting zero-area and full-disk cases.

= Circular arc
{parent=Circle}
{title2=$s=R\theta$}

A <circular arc> is a connected portion of a <circle>. For <radius> $R>0$ and subtended central <angle> $0<\theta<2\pi$ measured in <radians>, its <arc length> is $R\theta$. Two distinct endpoints determine two <circular arcs>: when they are not opposite, the minor arc has $\theta<\pi$ and the major arc has $\theta>\pi$. Opposite endpoints determine two <semicircles>. The <straight line> segment joining the endpoints is a <chord>, rather than the curved <circular arc> itself.

= Circular sector
{parent=Circle}
{wiki}
{title2=$A=\theta R^2/2$}

A <circular sector> is the portion of a disc between two radii and the connecting <circle> arc. If its <radius> is $R$ and its central <angle> is $\theta$ <radians>, its <area> is
$$
\boxed{A=\frac12\theta R^2.}
$$
Indeed, <polar coordinates> give $A=\int_0^\theta\int_0^Rr\,dr\,d\phi$. The full disc corresponds to $\theta=2\pi$, a <semicircle> to $\theta=\pi$, and a quarter-disc to $\theta=\pi/2$. In a solid with horizontal sector cross-sections, <radius> $R(z)$ and <angle> $\theta(z)$, its <volume> is $\tfrac12\int\theta(z)R(z)^2\,dz$. Holding the <angles> and heights fixed while scaling every <radius> by a factor $s$ scales that <volume> by $s^2$.

= Semicircle
{parent=Circle}
{wiki}

Half of a <circle>, with endpoints at opposite ends of a diameter. For radius $R>0$, the upper semicircle in the <complex plane> is parametrized by $z=Re^{it}$, $0\leq t\leq\pi$, going counterclockwise from $R$ to $-R$.

= Chord of a circle
{parent=Circle}
{title2=$L=2\sqrt{r^2-d^2}$}

= Chord
{synonym}

A chord joins two points of a <circle> by a straight segment. If the circle has radius $r$ and the chord midpoint lies a distance $d$ from its center, the perpendicular bisector and <Pythagorean theorem> give the displayed length. The length exceeds that of an inscribed <equilateral triangle> precisely when $d<r/2$.

= Reflection symmetry of a complex circle equation
{parent=Circle}
{title2=$z\mapsto (\beta/\bar\beta)\bar z$}

For real $\alpha\ne0,\gamma$ and complex $\beta\ne0$, completing the square in $\alpha|z|^2-\beta\bar z-\bar\beta z+\gamma=0$ gives center $\beta/\alpha$ and radius $\sqrt{|\beta|^2-\alpha\gamma}/|\alpha|$, when the radicand is nonnegative. The displayed map is reflection in the line through $0$ and $\beta$: it preserves the <complex modulus>, fixes the center, and squares to the identity. Hence it maps the <circle> onto itself, including a radius-zero degeneration.

= Maximum separation of two circles
{parent=Circle}
{title2=$m(C_1,C_2)=|c_1-c_2|+r_1+r_2$}

For two nonempty planar <circles> with centers $c_i$ and radii $r_i\ge0$, the greatest <Euclidean distance> between their points is the displayed expression. The <triangle inequality> gives the upper bound. When the centers differ, choose the two boundary points outward along the line of centers to attain it; when they coincide, choose opposite radial directions. For any third nonempty <circle> and a point $u$ on it, $|z-w|\le|z-u|+|u-w|$ proves $m(C_1,C_2)\le m(C_1,C_3)+m(C_2,C_3)$. This is not a <metric>, since $m(C,C)=2r$ for a positive-radius <circle>.

= Steiner chain
{c}
{parent=Circle}

= Steiner necklace
{synonym}
{c}

A cyclic chain of distinct <circles> between two disjoint <circle> boundaries, each tangent to both boundaries and its two neighbours. In concentric coordinates with inner radius $\rho$ and outer radius $R$, the chain <circles> have radius $r=(R-\rho)/2$ and centre distance $d=(R+\rho)/2$. A simple disjoint chain with $n\geq3$ closes when $r/d=\sin(\pi/n)$.

= Steiner porism
{c}
{parent=Steiner chain}

= Steiner's porism
{c}
{synonym}

If a simple <Steiner chain> closes after $n\geq3$ <circles>, rotating the starting <circle> between concentric boundaries gives another closed $n$-<circle> chain. Applying a <Möbius transformation> proves the corresponding statement for arbitrary boundary <circles>. Construction must stay in the same intervening family and proceed without reversing the chosen direction. Merely choosing arbitrary <circles> tangent to the preceding <circle> permits backtracking and does not assert closure.

= Tangency locus of a Steiner chain
{parent=Steiner chain}
{title2=$|z|=\sqrt{R\rho}$}

For a <Steiner chain> between concentric <circles>, tangencies of neighbouring <circles> are midpoints of their centres and lie on the <circle> of radius $\sqrt{R\rho}$. Under a <Möbius transformation>, their locus is a <generalized circle>, which may be a line. An ordinary-<circle> conclusion requires the pole of the inverse transformation to avoid the original locus.

= Circle from an affine complex-modulus equation
{parent=Circle}

An equation $|az+b\overline z|=ux+v$, with $z=x+iy$ and real coefficients, becomes $(a+b)^2x^2+(a-b)^2y^2=(ux+v)^2$ after squaring. If $(a+b)^2-u^2=(a-b)^2>0$, completing the square gives a <circle>. The original locus is only the part where $ux+v\geq0$. Checking this sign on the candidate <circle> prevents extraneous points introduced by squaring. This method combines the <complex modulus> with elementary conic geometry.

= Radius
{parent=Circle}
{title2=$r$}

The radius of a <circle> or <sphere> is the <Euclidean distance> from its centre to its boundary. The diameter is twice the radius.

= Pi
{title2=$\pi$}
{c}
{parent=Circle}
{wiki}

Pi is the ratio of a Euclidean circle's circumference to its diameter.

= Chudnovsky algorithm
{parent=Pi}
{c}

= Chudnovsky series
{parent=Chudnovsky algorithm}
{c}

= Wallis product
{parent=Pi}
{c}
{wiki}
{title2=$\pi/2=\prod_{j\geq1}\frac{4j^2}{4j^2-1}$}

The <Wallis product> is
$$
\frac\pi2=\prod_{j=1}^{\infty}\frac{(2j)^2}{(2j-1)(2j+1)}.
$$
Its finite product is $W_n=2^{4n}(n!)^4/[(2n+1)((2n)!)^2]$. The <Wallis integrals> give $2W_n/\pi=I_{2n+1}/I_{2n}\to1$, proving both convergence of the product and its value. The strict integral bounds also give $\pi n/(2n+1)<W_n<\pi/2$.

= Proof that π is irrational
{parent=Pi}
{wiki}

= Irrationality of pi
{c}
{synonym}

Assuming $\pi=a/b$ and integrating a suitably normalized polynomial times $\sin x$ produces a positive integer that tends to zero as the polynomial degree grows. This contradiction proves that $\pi$ is an <irrational number>.

= Circumference
{parent=Circle}
{title2=$C=2\pi r$}

The circumference of a <circle> is its boundary <arc length>. For <radius> $r$, it equals $2\pi r$. This growing boundary length accounts for the radial factor in the <Rayleigh distribution>.

= Wedge sum
{title2=$X\vee Y$}
{parent=Topological space}
{wiki}

The wedge sum of pointed spaces identifies all their basepoints. In particular, a wedge of $m$ circles has first homology $\mathbb Z^m$.

= Pinch map
{parent=Wedge sum}
{title2=$M\mathbin\#N\to M\vee N$}

A pinch map collapses a separating subspace to a point so that the quotient splits into a <wedge sum>. For a <connected sum of oriented manifolds>, collapsing the separating boundary sphere gives a map $M\mathbin\#N\to M\vee N$. The two wedge projections have <degree of a continuous mapping> one with compatible orientations. Consequently their pullbacks identify the two top <orientation classes>, while positive-degree classes from different summands have zero <cup product>.

= Wedge of two circles
{title2=$S^1\vee S^1$}
{parent=Wedge sum}

The wedge of two circles is a graph with one vertex and two loop edges. Its <fundamental group> is the free group on the two oriented loops.

= Inclusion map
{parent=Topological space}
{wiki}

For a subspace $A\subseteq X$, the inclusion map is the <injective function> $i:A\to X$ given by $i(a)=a$.

= Topology axiom
{parent=Topological space}

A family $\mathcal T$ of <subset>[subsets] of a <set> $X$ is a topology when $\varnothing,X\in\mathcal T$, every <union> of members of $\mathcal T$ belongs to $\mathcal T$, and every finite <intersection> of members of $\mathcal T$ belongs to $\mathcal T$.

= Open cover
{parent=Topological space}
{wiki}

An open cover of a subset $A$ is a family of open sets whose union contains $A$.

= Subcover
{parent=Open cover}
{title2=$J\subseteq I,\quad K\subseteq\bigcup_{j\in J}U_j$}

A <subcover> of an <open cover> $\{U_i:i\in I\}$ of a <subset> $K$ is a selection of its members whose <union> still contains $K$. The selected <open sets> retain their original sizes; selecting a <subcover> does not mean replacing them by smaller <open sets>.

= Finite subcover
{parent=Subcover}
{title2=$K\subseteq U_{i_1}\cup\cdots\cup U_{i_m}$}

A <finite subcover> is a <subcover> with finitely many members, including possibly none when $K$ is the <empty set>. Requiring a <finite subcover> for every <open cover> is the definition of <compactness>.

= Affine open cover
{parent=Open cover}
{title2=$X=\bigcup_iU_i,\quad U_i\cong\operatorname{Spec}A_i$}

An affine open cover of a <scheme> is an <open cover> by <affine open subsets>. Every <scheme> has such a cover by definition. A <quasi-compact scheme> has a finite affine open cover. Whether the intersections are affine is a further condition, supplied in particular by separatedness over an affine base.

= Acyclic cover
{parent=Open cover}

An open cover is acyclic for a specified <sheaf> if its restriction to every nonempty finite intersection has zero higher <sheaf cohomology>. The <acyclic cover theorem> then identifies the cover's <Čech cohomology> with sheaf cohomology. On a <separated scheme>, an affine open cover is acyclic for every <quasi-coherent sheaf>.

= Quotient topology
{parent=Topology}
{wiki}

The <quotient topology> turns an instruction to glue points together into a precise <topological space>. Start with a <topological space> $(X,\tau_X)$ and an <equivalence relation> $\sim$. The <quotient set> $X/{\sim}$ consists of <equivalence classes>, and its <canonical projection of a quotient set> is
$$
q:X\longrightarrow X/{\sim},\qquad q(x)=[x].
$$
The <quotient topology> is defined by the rule using <preimages> of <open sets>:
$$
\tau_q=\{U\subseteq X/{\sim}:q^{-1}(U)\in\tau_X\}.
$$
Thus a <subset> of the <quotient space> is <open> if and only if its entire <preimage> is <open> in $X$. Each <fiber> $q^{-1}(\{[x]\})$ is the whole <equivalence class> $[x]$, so the <preimage> includes every point glued to any of its members.<Point-Set Topology by Romyar Sharifi>[§2.3, Definitions 2.3.1 and 2.3.7 and Lemma 2.3.6]{ref}

This is the finest <topology> making $q$ a <continuous map>: every <topology> with that property can contain only <subsets> whose <preimages> are <open>. It really is a <topology>, because <preimages> preserve the <empty set>, the whole <set>, arbitrary <unions>, and finite <intersections>. The gluing example below shows why checking a complete <preimage> matters, especially at the seam.

The <quotient topology> also makes <continuous maps> out of a glued space easy to construct. A <continuous map> $f:X\to Z$ descends to a unique <continuous map> $\bar f:X/{\sim}\to Z$ exactly when it is constant on every <fiber> of $q$, meaning $x\sim y\Rightarrow f(x)=f(y)$. Define $\bar f([x])=f(x)$; the <equivalence class representative> cannot affect the answer. For every <open set> $V\subseteq Z$,
$$
q^{-1}\bigl(\bar f^{-1}(V)\bigr)=f^{-1}(V).
$$
The right side is <open>, so the <quotient topology> makes $\bar f^{-1}(V)$ <open>. Conversely, if $f=\bar f\circ q$ for a <continuous map> $\bar f$, then <composition of continuous functions> makes $f$ <continuous>, and equal <equivalence classes> force equal values. This is the <universal property of the quotient topology>. <Topology and Groups/3.02 Quotient topology: continuous maps>{ref}

A <surjective> <continuous map> is not automatically a <quotient map>. On the same two-point <set> $D=\{a,b\}$, take the <identity map> from the <discrete topology> to the <indiscrete topology>. It is <continuous> and <surjective>, but $\{a\}$ is not <open> in the target even though its <preimage> is <open> in the domain. The <quotient topology> for this <identity map> would be the <discrete topology>. Thus <continuity> supplies only one direction of the rule using <preimages> of <open sets>; a <quotient map> requires both.

= Closed equivalence relation with an open quotient map
{parent=Quotient topology}

If an <equivalence relation> is closed in a space's square and the <quotient map> is open, the <quotient> is <Hausdorff>. An inequivalent pair has an open rectangle avoiding the relation. The two factor <images> are open <quotient> neighborhoods and are disjoint, since an <intersection> would give equivalent representatives in that rectangle. Conversely, a <Hausdorff> <quotient> always has a closed <equivalence relation> by pulling back its closed diagonal.

= Hausdorff reflection by continuous maps
{c}
{parent=Quotient topology}
{title2=$x\sim y\iff f(x)=f(y)\text{ for every continuous map to a Hausdorff space}$}

For a <topological space> $X$, the displayed relation is an <equivalence relation>. Give its quotient the <quotient topology>. Every inverse image under a continuous map from $X$ to a <Hausdorff space> is a <saturation of a subset>. Distinct quotient classes can be distinguished by some such map; disjoint open neighborhoods of their images pull back to disjoint saturated open sets, whose quotient images are open. Thus the quotient is Hausdorff. Each continuous map from $X$ to a Hausdorff space factors uniquely through this quotient, and the factor is continuous by the quotient topology. This is its universal reflection property.

= Positive orbit separation implies a Hausdorff quotient
{parent=Quotient topology}

Suppose a <group> acts by <isometries> on a <metric space> $X$, and for points $a,b$ in different <orbits> one has $\inf_g d(a,gb)>0$. Then the <orbit> space with its <quotient topology> is <Hausdorff>. Indeed, choose $\varepsilon$ less than one third of this positive infimum and saturate the <open> radius-$\varepsilon$ balls about $a$ and $b$ by the <action>. These saturated sets are <open> because every acting <isometry> is a <homeomorphism>. Their translates cannot meet: their centres stay at least the given positive <distance> apart. Their disjoint <open> images in the quotient separate the two <orbit> points, since the preimage of each image is its saturated set. This applies to the <glide reflection> $(x,y)\mapsto(x+1,-y)$ on a <closed> strip: the <distance> from one point to the translates of another tends to infinity as the <integer> translation grows, so two distinct <orbits> have a positive minimum <distance>.

= Quotient topology of a cofinite space
{parent=Quotient topology}

A <quotient space> of a space carrying the <cofinite topology> has a <topology> contained in the <cofinite topology> on its underlying <quotient set>. If $q:X\to Y$ is the <quotient map> and $U$ is a nonempty <open subset> of $Y$, its <preimage> has finite complement. Since $q$ is <surjective>, the complement of $U$ is the image of that finite complement, hence is finite. Equality need not hold: partition $\mathbb Z$ into its even and odd integers. Both classes are infinite, so their two-point <quotient space> has the <indiscrete topology>, whereas the <cofinite topology> on a finite set is the <discrete topology>.

= Circle modulo reflection
{parent=Quotient topology}
{title2=$S^1/(z\sim\overline z)\cong[0,1]$}

Identify points of the <circle> having the same real coordinate, equivalently points related by <complex conjugation>. The <continuous> <surjection> $h(z)=(1+\operatorname{Re}z)/2$ has exactly these <equivalence classes> as its fibers. It induces a <continuous bijection> from the <quotient space> to the <closed interval>. The <quotient space> is <compact> as a <continuous image of a compact space>, while the <closed interval> is <Hausdorff>, so the <compact-to-Hausdorff continuous bijection theorem> makes the induced map a <homeomorphism>. The two fixed points of the reflection become the two interval endpoints.

= Collapsing a subset to a point
{parent=Quotient topology}
{title2=$q_A:X\to X/A$}

For a nonempty <subset> $A$ of a <topological space> $X$, identify all points of $A$ and leave each point of $X\setminus A$ in its own <equivalence class>. The resulting <quotient space> is denoted $X/A$. For every <subset> $K\subseteq X$, its <saturation of a subset> is
$$
q_A^{-1}(q_A(K))=
\begin{cases}
K,&K\cap A=\varnothing,\\
K\cup A,&K\cap A\ne\varnothing.
\end{cases}
$$
The <quotient topology> therefore gives a useful test: $q_A(K)$ is a <closed set> if and only if the displayed <saturation of a subset> is a <closed set> of $X$. In particular, every <closed set> disjoint from $A$ has <closed> image, even if $A$ is not <closed>. When $A$ is <closed>, $q_A$ is a <closed map>, because both possibilities in the formula are then <closed sets> for <closed> $K$.

= Noncompactness after collapsing a countable real subset
{parent=Collapsing a subset to a point}
{title2=$A\subseteq\mathbb R\text{ countable}\implies\mathbb R/A\text{ not compact}$}

Choose $b_n\in(n,n+1/2)\setminus A$, possible because each interval is uncountable. Every tail $K_n=\{b_k:k\geq n\}$ is <closed> and disjoint from $A$. Therefore $U_n=\mathbb R\setminus K_n$ is an open saturated set for <collapsing a subset to a point>, so its image in the <quotient topology> is open. These increasing images cover the quotient, but no finite selection covers it, because the largest selected image still misses an infinite tail of distinct singleton classes. This is an <open cover> with no <finite subcover>, proving noncompactness. The argument does not require the countable subset to be closed or nondense. For the empty subset, read the quotient as the original line.

= Saturation of a subset
{parent=Quotient topology}
{title2=$\operatorname{Sat}(A)=p^{-1}(p(A))$}

For the <canonical projection of a quotient set> $p:X\to X/{\sim}$, the saturation of $A\subseteq X$ is the union of all <equivalence classes> meeting $A$, namely $p^{-1}(p(A))$. A subset is called saturated when it equals its saturation. The <quotient topology> says that $p(A)$ is <closed> exactly when the saturation of $A$ is <closed>, and similarly for openness. A <closed set> need not have <closed> saturation: collapsing $(0,1)\subset\mathbb R$ to one point makes the saturation of $\{1/2\}$ equal to $(0,1)$. Hence a <quotient map> need not be a <closed map>.

= Quotient topological space
{parent=Quotient topology}
{title2=$X/{\sim}$}

= Quotient space
{synonym}

A <quotient topological space> is a <quotient set> equipped with the <quotient topology>. For a concrete gluing, give the <closed interval> $I=[0,1]$ its usual <subspace topology>, identify $0\sim1$, and leave every interior point in its own <singleton> <equivalence class>. The resulting <quotient space> is a <circle>: the two endpoints become one point, while interior points remain distinct.

\Image[quotient-interval-endpoints-circle.png]
{title=Gluing the interval endpoints produces a circle, with matching colors showing the projection}

The exact <continuous map> to the <unit circle> in the plane is
$$
p(t)=\bigl(\cos(2\pi t),\sin(2\pi t)\bigr),\qquad
\bar p([t])=p(t).
$$
Here <sine> and <cosine> give $p(0)=p(1)=(1,0)$, and $p(s)=p(t)$ holds exactly when $s=t$ or $\{s,t\}=\{0,1\}$. Thus $p$ induces a <bijection> $\bar p:I/{\sim}\to S^1$. <Topology and Groups/3.01 Quotient topology>{ref} The <universal property of the quotient topology> makes $\bar p$ <continuous>. The <quotient of a compact space> is <compact>, and the <unit circle> is <Hausdorff>, so the <compact-to-Hausdorff continuous bijection theorem> makes $\bar p$ a <homeomorphism>. This verifies the <topology>, not merely the point identifications.

To inspect an <open> <neighbourhood> of the seam, choose $0<\varepsilon<1/2$ and let $U_\varepsilon=p([0,\varepsilon)\cup(1-\varepsilon,1])$. Its full <preimage> is
$$
p^{-1}(U_\varepsilon)=[0,\varepsilon)\cup(1-\varepsilon,1].
$$
Both pieces are <open> in the <subspace topology> on $I$, so $U_\varepsilon$ is <open> in the <circle>. In contrast, $A=[0,\varepsilon)$ is <open> in $I$, but
$$
p^{-1}\bigl(p(A)\bigr)=[0,\varepsilon)\cup\{1\}
$$
is not <open> in $I$: it contains $1$ without any <neighbourhood> of $1$. Hence $p(A)$ is not <open> in the <circle>. A <quotient map> can therefore fail to be an <open map>.

\Image[quotient-circle-open-preimages.png]
{title=An open neighbourhood crosses both sides of the glued seam; a one-sided arc fails the full-preimage test}

For a <continuous function> $f:I\to\mathbb R$, the only gluing condition is $f(0)=f(1)$. For example, $f(t)=\cos(2\pi t)$ defines a <continuous function> on the <circle>, whereas $f(t)=t$ does not. This lets us use an <interval> as a coordinate for a <periodic function> while making the seam invisible. More general <quotient spaces> encode glued geometric pieces in the same way: describe <continuous maps> before gluing and check that their values agree on every <equivalence class>.

The <Hausdorff> property need not survive a <quotient topology>. In the <non-Hausdorff quotient of the real line by rational translation>, $x\sim y$ means $x-y\in\mathbb Q$. A nonempty <open> <preimage> $W$ must contain all translates by <rational numbers> of any <open interval> inside it. The <density of the rational numbers> then forces $W=\mathbb R$: for any $y$, some <rational number> $r$ has $y-r$ in that <interval>. Thus this <quotient space> has the <indiscrete topology>. The <irrationality of the square root of two> gives distinct <equivalence classes> for $0$ and $\sqrt2$, so this <quotient space> is not <Hausdorff>. Unlike the endpoint-gluing <circle>, this gluing identifies a <dense subset> with each point.

= Sphere with its equator collapsed
{parent=Quotient topological space}
{title2=$S^2/S^1\cong S^2\vee S^2$}

Collapse the equator of a <sphere> $S^2$ to a single <equivalence class> and leave every other point in its own class. The two closed hemispheres are <disks> sharing that equator. A disk with its boundary collapsed is a sphere, so the two hemisphere quotients are spheres sharing just one point: their <wedge sum>. An explicit map sends height $t\in[0,1]$ and longitude $\theta$ on the upper hemisphere to $(1-\cos\pi t,\sin\pi t\cos\theta,\sin\pi t\sin\theta)$, and sends the lower hemisphere to the sphere centred at $(-1,0,0)$ by reversing the first coordinate. The equator goes to the common origin and the poles go to $(\pm2,0,0)$. This map is continuous and has precisely the collapsed equator as its only nonsingleton fibre. Its induced map is a <homeomorphism> by the <compact-to-Hausdorff continuous bijection theorem>.

= Compact Hausdorff quotient by a closed equivalence relation
{parent=Quotient topological space}

Let $X$ be a <compact Hausdorff space> and $R\subseteq X\times X$ a closed <equivalence relation>. The <quotient space> $X/R$ is compact and Hausdorff. Indeed, the saturation $R[C]$ of any closed $C\subseteq X$ is the projection of the compact set $R\cap(C\times X)$, so is closed. Thus the quotient map is a <closed map>.

Two different equivalence classes are disjoint compact subsets of $X$ and have disjoint open neighbourhoods $U,V$. To see the latter assertion, separate each pair of points by disjoint neighbourhoods and use compactness twice to extract finite covers. Now $U'=X\setminus R[X\setminus U]$ and $V'=X\setminus R[X\setminus V]$ are open saturated subsets containing the respective classes, and lie inside $U,V$. Their quotient images are disjoint open neighbourhoods, proving the <Hausdorff condition>. Compactness follows from the continuous quotient map. This applies to finite gluing along closed boundary pieces when the generated equivalence relation is closed; that last condition must be checked.

= Quotient map
{parent=Quotient topology}
{wiki}

A quotient map is a surjective continuous map $q:X\to Y$ for which $U\subseteq Y$ is open exactly when $q^{-1}(U)$ is open.

= Product of a quotient map with the unit interval
{parent=Quotient map}
{title2=$q\times\operatorname{id}_{[0,1]}$}

If $q:E\to B$ is a <quotient map>, then $q\times\operatorname{id}_{[0,1]}$ is a <quotient map>. More generally the second factor may be any <locally compact space> that is <Hausdorff>. To prove the assertion, let $W\subseteq B\times[0,1]$ have open preimage $O$, and take $(b,t)\in W$. Choose $e\in q^{-1}(b)$ and a compact <neighbourhood> $K$ of $t$ with $\{e\}\times K\subseteq O$. The set $E_K=\{u:\{u\}\times K\subseteq O\}$ is open by <compactness> of $K$, and is saturated under $q$ because membership in $O$ depends only on $q(u)$ and the second coordinate. Hence $q(E_K)$ is open by the <quotient topology>. The rectangle $q(E_K)\times\operatorname{int}K$ is a <neighbourhood> of $(b,t)$ contained in $W$, proving that $W$ is open. This justifies parameterized <homotopies> defined on <quotient spaces>.

= Universal property of the quotient topology
{parent=Quotient topology}

If $q:X\to X/R$ is the quotient map and a continuous map $f:X\to Y$ is constant on each equivalence class, there is a unique continuous map $F:X/R\to Y$ satisfying $Fq=f$. It is given by $F([x])=f(x)$; continuity follows because
$$
q^{-1}(F^{-1}(U))=f^{-1}(U)
$$
for every open $U\subseteq Y$.

= Non-Hausdorff quotient of the real line by rational translation
{parent=Quotient topology}

Identify $x,y\in\mathbb R$ when $x-y\in\mathbb Q$. Every nonempty saturated open subset of $\mathbb R$ meets every other one because rational translates of any open interval meet any prescribed nonempty open interval. The quotient $\mathbb R/\mathbb Q$ has multiple points but no two nonempty disjoint open subsets, so it is not Hausdorff.

= Square quotient model of the two-sphere
{parent=Quotient topology}

Identify the vertical sides of $[0,1]^2$, then collapse each horizontal side separately to a point. The resulting quotient is homeomorphic to $S^2$ through
$$
(x,y)\longmapsto
\bigl(\sin(\pi y)\cos(2\pi x),
\sin(\pi y)\sin(2\pi x),\cos(\pi y)\bigr).
$$
Equivalently, the quotient is the suspension of the circle.

= Open set
{parent=Topology}
{wiki}
{title2=$U\in\tau$}

= Open subset
{synonym}

= Open
{synonym}

An open set is a member of the <topology> on a <topological space>. In a <metric space>, a <subset> $U$ is an <open set> precisely when every $x\in U$ has an <open ball> $B(x,r)$ contained in $U$ for some $r>0$. For $U=(0,1)$, one can take $r=\tfrac12\min(x,1-x)$. At $x=0.2$, this gives $r=0.1$ and $B(x,r)=(0.1,0.3)\subset U$. The radius depends on the point; no uniform radius is required. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-100b-real-analysis-spring-2025/resources/ocw_18100b-lec13-2025apr03_mp4/[MIT Real Analysis, Lecture 13]{ref}

Openness depends on the ambient <topological space>. In $A=[0,1]$ with its <subspace topology>, $[0,1/2)=A\cap(-1,1/2)$ is an <open set> in $A$, although it is not an <open set> in the <real line>. Similarly, the whole <closed interval> $A$ is both an <open set> and a <closed set> in itself. These relative <open sets> are what the <continuity> test uses for maps involving a <subset> with its <subspace topology>.

Arbitrary <intersections> of <open sets> need not be <open sets>: $\bigcap_{n\geq1}(-1/n,1/n)=\{0\}$, a <singleton> that is not an <open set> in the usual <real line>. Any point $x\ne0$ is excluded once $n>1/|x|$. This is why the <topology axiom> specifies finite <intersections>.

= Domain
{parent=Open set}
{wiki}
{disambiguate=mathematical analysis}

= Domain
{synonym}

= Domain of mathematical analysis
{synonym}

A domain is a nonempty connected <open set> in the ambient space under discussion. In <complex analysis> this normally means an open connected subset of $\mathbb C$. A <star-shaped set> that is open is a domain, but a domain need not be a <simply connected domain> and can have a <period obstruction to a holomorphic antiderivative>.

= Clopen set
{parent=Open set}
{wiki}

= Clopen
{synonym}

A clopen set is a subset that is both <open set>[open] and <closed set>[closed].

= Open interval
{parent=Open set}

This is the open-boundary case of an <interval>.

An open interval in the real line is a set $(a,b)=\{x:a<x<b\}$, allowing either endpoint to be infinite.

= Nondegenerate interval
{parent=Open interval}

A nondegenerate interval contains more than one point; for an interval with finite endpoints, this means that its endpoints are distinct.

= Neighbourhood
{parent=Open set}
{disambiguate=mathematics}
{wiki}

= Neighbourhood
{synonym}

= Neighborhood
{synonym}

A neighbourhood of a point is a set containing an open set that contains that point.

= Open neighbourhood
{parent=Neighbourhood (mathematics)}
{title2=$x\in U\in\mathcal T$}

= Open neighborhood
{synonym}

An <open neighbourhood> of a point $x$ in a <topological space> $(X,\mathcal T)$ is an <open set> $U$ containing $x$. A general <neighbourhood> need only contain an <open neighbourhood>; it need not itself be <open>. A finite <intersection> of <open neighbourhoods> of the same point is again an <open neighbourhood>, by the <topology axioms>.

= Neighbourhood system
{parent=Neighbourhood (mathematics)}
{wiki}

= Neighborhood system
{synonym}

The <neighbourhood system> at a point consists of all its <neighbourhoods>. A <neighbourhood basis> is a subfamily cofinal under reverse inclusion.

= Neighbourhood basis
{parent=Neighbourhood system}

= Neighborhood basis
{synonym}

= Neighbourhood bases
{synonym}

= Neighborhood bases
{synonym}

A neighbourhood basis at a point is a family of neighbourhoods such that every neighbourhood of the point contains one of them.

= Open ball
{parent=Open set}

In a <metric space>, the <open ball> of radius $r>0$ about $x$ is $\{y:d(x,y)<r\}$; it is the open-boundary convention for a <ball (mathematics)>.

= Punctured ball
{parent=Open ball}
{title2=$B_r(p)\setminus\{p\}$}

A punctured ball is an <open ball> with its centre removed. In the <real line> it has two <connected components>. In <Euclidean space> of dimension $n\geq2$ it is <path-connected>: for any two points, choose a third point inside the ball outside the two <lines> through the centre and those points. Such a point exists since two <lines> do not cover an <open ball> in dimension at least two. The two <line segments> joining through that point stay inside the ball, which is a <convex set>, and avoid its centre. They form a <continuous path>.

= Annulus
{parent=Open set}
{disambiguate=mathematics}
{wiki}

= Annulus
{synonym}

= Annuli
{synonym}

An annulus is the region between two concentric circles.

= Fixed-point-free rotation of an annulus
{parent=Annulus (mathematics)}

Rotation through any nonzero angle defines a continuous self-map of a circular annulus with no fixed point. Hence an annulus does not have the <fixed-point property>.

= Disk
{disambiguate=mathematics}
{parent=Topology}
{title2=$D_R$}
{wiki}

= Disk
{synonym}

= Euclidean disk
{c}
{synonym}

A planar <Euclidean disk> consists of the points within a fixed radius of a centre, with either the open or closed boundary convention. The closed disk centered at the origin is $D_R=\{x\in\mathbb R^2:|x|\leq R\}$ and has <area> $\pi R^2$. It is the two-dimensional closed Euclidean ball; the general <closed disc> may have any dimension.

= Semicircular disk
{parent=Disk (mathematics)}
{title2=$\{(x,y):x^2+y^2\leq a^2,\ y\geq0\}$}

A semicircular disk is one half of a <disk> cut along a diameter, including its interior. It is a <circular sector> of angle $\pi$, with <area> $\pi a^2/2$. Source <polar coordinates> use $0\leq r\leq a$, $0\leq\theta\leq\pi$, and <area element> $r\,dr\,d\theta$. Its boundary consists of a <semicircle> and a diameter; the <semicircle> alone is a <curve> and has no planar interior.

= Open disk
{parent=Disk (mathematics)}
{title2=$B^2=\{v\in\mathbb R^2:|v|<1\}$}

The <open disk> is the interior of a planar <disk>. Any such <open disk> is <homeomorphic> to $\mathbb R^2$, and in particular is <contractible> and <simply connected>.

= Lens-shaped intersection of two disks
{parent=Disk (mathematics)}
{title2=$A=2r^2\arccos[d/(2r)]-(d/2)\sqrt{4r^2-d^2}$}

For two <disks> of equal radius $r$ whose centres are separated by $0\leq d\leq2r$, their intersection consists of two circular segments. Each segment is a sector of angle $2\theta$ minus an isosceles triangle, where $\cos\theta=d/(2r)$. Hence its total <area> is
$$
A=2r^2\theta-r^2\sin(2\theta)
=2r^2\arccos\frac{d}{2r}-\frac d2\sqrt{4r^2-d^2}.
$$
The limiting cases are $A=\pi r^2$ at $d=0$ and $A=0$ at $d=2r$. This geometric region is distinct from an <optical lens>.

= Open disc
{parent=Disk (mathematics)}

The open disc with centre $z$ and radius $r>0$ is $\{w:|w-z|<r\}$.

= Unit disc
{title2=$\mathbb D$}
{parent=Open disc}

= Open unit disk
{synonym}

The unit disc in the <complex plane> is $\mathbb D=\{z\in\mathbb C:|z|<1\}$.

= Automorphism of the unit disk
{parent=Unit disc}
{title2=$\phi(z)=e^{i\theta}\frac{z-a}{1-\overline a z}$}

= Automorphisms of the unit disk
{synonym}

= Disk automorphism
{synonym}

= Disc automorphism
{synonym}

A <biholomorphism> of the unit disk to itself. Every such map has the form $e^{i\theta}(z-a)/(1-\overline a z)$ with $|a|<1$. These maps normalize an extremal <univalent function> in the proof of the <Riemann mapping theorem>.

= Boundary-angle derivative of a disk automorphism
{parent=Automorphism of the unit disk}
{title2=$f_w(e^{i\theta})=e^{i\phi_w(\theta)},\qquad\phi_w'(\theta)=\frac{1-|w|^2}{|e^{i\theta}-w|^2}$}

For $|w|<1$, the <disk automorphism> $f_w(z)=(z-w)/(1-\overline wz)$ maps the boundary circle to itself. If $g(\theta)$ is a continuous argument of $w-e^{i\theta}$, direct conjugation gives $\phi_w(\theta)=-\theta+2g(\theta)$ modulo $2\pi$. Differentiating the argument gives
$$
g'(\theta)=\frac{1-\operatorname{Re}(we^{-i\theta})}{|w-e^{i\theta}|^2},
$$
and substitution yields the displayed positive derivative. Its integral over one circuit is $2\pi$, so the boundary map is orientation preserving of degree one. Transforming a uniform exit angle at the centre by its inverse gives the <harmonic measure> density $(2\pi)^{-1}\phi_w'(\theta)$ for a <planar Brownian motion> started at $w$. This is the <Poisson kernel for the unit disk> with its probability normalization.

= Topological disc
{parent=Topology}
{title2=$D^n$}

= Disc
{synonym}

An $n$-dimensional topological disc is a space homeomorphic to a Euclidean ball in $\mathbb R^n$, with its open or closed convention specified. A <closed disc> is homeomorphic to $D^n=\{x\in\mathbb R^n:|x|\leq1\}$. The planar <disk> is the Euclidean two-dimensional instance, rather than the whole abstract topological class.

= Closed disc
{parent=Topological disc}

= Closed disk
{synonym}

A closed $n$-disc is a space homeomorphic to $D^n=\{x\in\mathbb R^n:|x|\leq1\}$; its boundary is the sphere $S^{n-1}$.

= Level set
{parent=Topology}
{wiki}

A level set of a function $f$ is a set of the form $\{x:f(x)=c\}$.

= Level surface
{parent=Level set}
{title2=$f(x,y,z)=c$}

A <level set> of a real-valued function on three-dimensional space. Where the function is continuously differentiable and its <gradient> is nonzero, the <regular level set theorem> makes this a smooth surface with <normal vector> parallel to the gradient. The equation alone can also have singular points, or fail to describe a two-dimensional surface at all.

= Level sets of a linear numerator over a radial quadratic
{parent=Level set}
{title2=$f(x)=(a\cdot x)/(1+\|x\|^2)$}

For a fixed nonzero $a\in\mathbb R^n$, a nonzero <level set> $f(x)=h$ is the sphere
$$
\left\|x-\frac a{2h}\right\|^2=\frac{\|a\|^2}{4h^2}-1.
$$
This follows by completing the square after multiplying by the positive denominator. Nonzero levels exist only for $|h|\leq\|a\|/2$. The zero level is the <hyperplane> $a\cdot x=0$; the extreme levels shrink to points $x=\pm a/\|a\|$. The <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> gives $|f(x)|\leq\|a\|\|x\|/(1+\|x\|^2)\leq\|a\|/2$, so these extrema are global. At them the <Hessian matrix> is respectively $-\|a\|I/2$ and $+\|a\|I/2$. In two dimensions the spheres are circles whose centres all lie on the axis spanned by $a$.

= Level curve
{parent=Level set}
{title2=$f(x,y)=c$}

A <level curve> is the planar <level set> of a scalar function. Where the gradient does not vanish, the <implicit function theorem> makes it locally a smooth curve. At a <critical point>, branches may cross, narrow into a cusp or shrink to a point, so a plot should be interpreted with the local critical-point analysis.

= Contour plot
{parent=Level curve}

A <contour plot> displays several <level curves> of a real-valued <function> of two variables, usually with labels indicating their values. It represents a surface's height on a flat page. Closely spaced <level curves> indicate a large change per unit distance only when the gaps between their labeled values are comparable.

= Regular level set
{parent=Level set}
{title2=$M=f^{-1}(c),\quad Df_p\text{ surjective for every }p\in M$}

A <level set> at a <regular value> is a smooth <submanifold>. By the <regular level set theorem>, its codimension is the dimension of the target. For determinant-one real <symmetric matrices>, $D(\det)_B(C)=\operatorname{tr}(B^{-1}C)$ is nonzero because $C=B$ gives $n$, so the constraint has codimension one.

= Superlevel set
{parent=Level set}

A superlevel set of a real-valued function $f$ is a set of the form $\{x:f(x)\geq c\}$ or $\{x:f(x)>c\}$.

= Dense set
{parent=Topology}
{wiki}

= Dense subset
{synonym}

= Dense
{synonym}

A subset is dense when every nonempty <open set> meets it, equivalently when its closure is the whole ambient space.

= Cardinality of the family of dense subsets of the reals
{parent=Dense set}
{title2=$2^{2^{\aleph_0}}$}

There are $2^{\mathfrak c}$ dense subsets of $\mathbb R$, where $\mathfrak c=|\mathbb R|$. For a fixed <Cantor set> $C$ of size $\mathfrak c$, its complement $D$ is dense, and $A\mapsto D\cup A$ injects $\mathcal P(C)$ into the family of dense subsets. The reverse injection is the inclusion into $\mathcal P(\mathbb R)$. The <Cantor-Schröder-Bernstein theorem> gives equality.

= Discrete space
{parent=Topology}
{wiki}

= Discrete topology
{synonym}

A topological space is discrete when every subset is an <open set>.

= Discrete subset
{parent=Discrete space}

= Discrete set
{synonym}

A subset of a <topological space> is discrete when its <subspace topology> is a <discrete topology>: every point has an ambient neighbourhood meeting the subset only in that point. A discrete subset need not be closed; for example $\{1/n:n\geq1\}$ in the real line accumulates at zero. The group structure makes a <discrete subgroup> of a Hausdorff <topological group> closed, an additional property used in discontinuity arguments.

= Closed discrete subset
{parent=Discrete subset}

A closed discrete subset $D$ of a <topological space> $X$ is a <closed set> whose <subspace topology> is the <discrete topology>. Every point of $D$ therefore has a <neighbourhood> meeting $D$ at that point alone. Closedness excludes all accumulation points outside $D$ as well.

In a plane <domain>, a closed <discrete subset> is exactly a set meeting every <compact subset> in finitely many points. This local finiteness is necessary for a nonzero <holomorphic function> to have the subset as its entire zero set. A merely isolated-point subset can have an accumulation point outside the subset but inside the domain, as $\{1/n:n\geq1\}\subset\mathbb C$ does.

// Destination: analysis.bigb

= Closed discrete subsets of compact spaces are finite
{parent=Closed discrete subset}

Let $D$ be a <closed discrete subset> of a <compact space> $X$. Closedness makes $D$ compact. Discreteness makes its singletons open in its <subspace topology>, so the singleton cover has a finite subcover. Thus $D$ is finite. Closedness is essential: the discrete set $\{1/n:n\geq1\}$ inside the compact interval $[0,1]$ is infinite and has the missing accumulation point zero.

= Discrete subsets of the real line are countable
{parent=Discrete subset}
{title2=$B\subseteq\mathbb R\text{ discrete}\Rightarrow|B|\le\aleph_0$}

Enumerate all intervals with rational endpoints, using the countability of the <rational numbers> and listing pairs of enumeration indices by increasing sum. If every point $b$ of $B$ is an <isolated point>, some such interval contains $b$ and no other point of $B$. Assign to $b$ the least index of such an interval. Distinct points cannot have the same index, so this gives an <injective function> into $\mathbb N$ and proves that $B$ is a <countable set>. The argument uses isolation in the <subspace topology>, not closedness: the <discrete subset> $\{1/n:n\ge1\}$ illustrates that an accumulation point outside $B$ is allowed.

= Connected-space zero-product dichotomy
{parent=Topology}

If continuous functions $f,g$ on a connected space satisfy $fg=0$ and never vanish simultaneously, then the disjoint open sets $\{f\ne0\}$ and $\{g\ne0\}$ cover the space. One is empty, so one function vanishes identically.

= Normal space
{parent=Topology}
{wiki}

= Normal topological space
{synonym}

A topological space is normal when every two disjoint closed subsets have disjoint open neighbourhoods.

= Hereditarily normal space
{parent=Normal space}

A space is hereditarily normal when every subspace is a <normal topological space>. This is stronger than normality: arbitrary subspaces need not be closed. Every <perfectly normal space> is hereditarily normal.

= Perfectly normal space
{parent=Normal space}

A <T1 space> is perfectly normal when it is normal and every closed subset is a <G-delta set>. Equivalently every closed subset is the <zero set of a continuous function> into $[0,1]$, using the <closed G-delta set as a zero set> theorem. If disjoint closed sets are the exact zero sets of $f,g$, then $f/(f+g)$ has those sets as its exact zero and one levels. Every subspace is normal, although the converse fails.

= Perfect normality implies hereditary normality
{parent=Perfectly normal space}

For disjoint closed sets $A,B$ in a subspace $Y$, their ambient closures $C,D$ satisfy $(C\cap D)\cap Y=\varnothing$. In a <perfectly normal space> choose continuous functions $f,g$ with exact zero sets $C,D$. The function $f/(f+g)$ is continuous on $X\setminus(C\cap D)$, which contains $Y$, and separates $A,B$. Disjoint inverse images of endpoint neighborhoods give normality of $Y$.

= Closed-neighborhood shrinking in a normal space
{parent=Normal space}
{title2=$F\subset W\subset\overline W\subset U$}

If $F$ is closed, $U$ is open, and $F\subset U$ in a <normal topological space>, separate $F$ from the closed complement $X\setminus U$ by disjoint open sets $W,V$. Then $\overline W\subset X\setminus V\subset U$. In particular, first separate two disjoint closed sets by disjoint open sets and then shrink each neighborhood this way: their closures are disjoint. No additional metric or compactness assumption is needed.

= Urysohn's lemma
{parent=Normal space}
{wiki}
{c}

= Urysohn lemma
{c}
{synonym}

If $A,B$ are disjoint closed subsets of a normal space, there is a continuous function $f:X\to[0,1]$ with $f|_A=0$ and $f|_B=1$.

= Urysohn function from closure-nested open sets
{parent=Urysohn's lemma}
{c}
{title2=$f(x)=\inf\{t:x\in U_t\}$}

Let <open sets> $U_t$ be indexed by a <dense subset> of $[0,1]$, with $\overline{U_s}\subseteq U_t$ for $s<t$. Taking the infimum of membership indices, with value one outside their <union>, gives a <continuous function>. The strict sublevel <sets> are <unions> of $U_t$; strict superlevel <sets> are <unions> of <complements> of $\overline{U_t}$. These identities prove <continuity>.

= Tietze extension theorem
{c}
{parent=Urysohn's lemma}
{wiki}

Every continuous real-valued function on a closed subspace of a normal space extends continuously to the whole space. If its values lie in a closed interval, the extension can be kept in that interval.

= Strict-range modification of a continuous extension
{parent=Tietze extension theorem}
{title2=$\widetilde f=(1-u)f$}

Suppose $Y$ is closed in a <normal space> $X$ and $|f|<1$ on $Y$ for a <continuous> real-valued $f$ on $X$. The <closed set> $Z=\{|f|\ge1\}$ misses $Y$. <Urysohn's lemma> supplies $u:X\to[0,1]$ equal to zero on $Y$ and one on $Z$. Then $(1-u)f$ agrees with $f$ on $Y$ and has range in $(-1,1)$. This permits the inverse transform $s\mapsto s/(1-|s|)$ in the proof of unbounded Tietze extension.

= Compact subsets of Tychonoff spaces are C-embedded
{parent=Tietze extension theorem}

A <continuous function> on a <compact subset> of a <Tychonoff space> extends to the whole space. Embed the space into a cube of closed unit intervals. The <Tychonoff theorem> makes the cube <compact> <Hausdorff> and thus normal. The <compact subset> is closed in that cube, so the <Tietze extension theorem> extends its bounded function to the cube. Restrict this extension to the embedded original space. Normality of the original space is unnecessary.

= Urysohn two-thirds approximation
{c}
{parent=Tietze extension theorem}
{title2=$\|h\|_\infty\leq M/3,\quad\|g-h|_C\|_\infty\leq2M/3$}

Let $C$ be closed in a <normal topological space> and $g:C\to[-M,M]$ continuous. Separate its closed level sets $g\leq-M/3$ and $g\geq M/3$ by <Urysohn's lemma> to obtain $h:X\to[-M/3,M/3]$ taking the corresponding endpoint values. Checking the outer and middle ranges gives the displayed bounds. Iteration on residuals gives a uniformly convergent series with total <supremum norm> at most $M$. The <uniform limit theorem> gives the bounded <Tietze extension theorem>.

= Metric Tietze two-thirds approximation
{parent=Tietze extension theorem}
{title2=$\|u\|_\infty\le M/3,\quad\|u|_Y-g\|_\infty\le2M/3$}

For a real function $g$ in the <bounded continuous functions> of norm $M$ on a closed subset $Y$ of a <metric space>, separate its closed level sets $A=\{g\le-M/3\}$ and $B=\{g\ge M/3\}$ by $u=(M/3)(d(\cdot,A)-d(\cdot,B))/(d(\cdot,A)+d(\cdot,B))$. The <distance to a set> functions make $u$ continuous with the displayed bounds. Empty level sets are handled by the constants $M/3$, $-M/3$ or $0$. Iterating on the residual yields a uniformly convergent continuous extension with no increase in the <supremum norm>.

= Closed G-delta set as a zero set
{parent=Normal space}

A closed subset $A$ of a normal space is a countable intersection of open sets exactly when it is the zero set of some continuous function $f:X\to[0,1]$.

= Compact space
{parent=Topology}
{wiki}

= Quasi-compact topological space
{synonym}

= Quasi-compactness
{synonym}

= Quasi-compact
{synonym}

= Compact subset
{synonym}

= Compact
{synonym}

= Compact set
{synonym}

= Compactness
{synonym}

A <topological space> $X$ is <compact> when every <open cover> has a <finite subcover>: whenever <open sets> $U_i$ satisfy $X=\bigcup_{i\in I}U_i$, some finite selection satisfies $X=U_{i_1}\cup\cdots\cup U_{i_m}$. A <subset> $K$ of an ambient <topological space> is <compact> when its <subspace topology> is <compact>; equivalently, every <open cover> of $K$ by ambient <open sets> has a <finite subcover>. The word *every* matters: finding one <finite subcover> of one <open cover> is not enough to establish <compactness>. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-101-analysis-ii-fall-2005/babd982be745679b6d691f78b1c18f53_lectures.pdf[MIT Analysis II, section 1.6 and Definition 1.23]{ref}

The <closed interval> $[0,1]$ is <compact> by the <Heine-Borel theorem>. For a concrete <open cover>, take the three <open intervals> $(-0.1,0.45)$, $(0.35,0.8)$, and $(0.7,1.1)$. Their <union> is $(-0.1,1.1)$, so they cover every point of $[0,1]$, including both endpoints. The overlaps have lengths $0.1$ and $0.1$, leaving no gaps. Each member is necessary for this particular <open cover>: removing them respectively leaves $0$, $0.5$, or $1$ uncovered.

\Image[compact-open-cover.png]
{title=A <finite subcover> of a <closed interval>, and an <open cover> with no <finite subcover>}

Above, hollow markers denote the excluded endpoints of the covering <open intervals>, while the solid endpoints belong to the covered <closed interval>. Below, each displayed <open interval> is one member of an infinite <open cover> of $(0,1)$; finitely many rows always leave points near zero uncovered. The rows are vertically separated for readability and all refer to the same <real line>.

The <open interval> $(0,1)$ is a <bounded set> but is not <compact>: the <open sets>
$$
U_n=(1/n,1),\qquad n\geq2,
$$
give an <open cover> of $(0,1)$, since every $0<x<1$ has $1/n<x$ for sufficiently large $n$. These are also <open sets> for the <subspace topology> on $(0,1)$. Any nonempty finite selection has a largest index $N$, so its <union> is just $(1/N,1)$ and misses the point $1/(N+1)$, which still belongs to $(0,1)$. Thus no <finite subcover> exists, however many members we select. The same family is not an <open cover> of $[0,1]$, since it misses both endpoints.

In a <metric space>, <compactness> is equivalent to <sequential compactness>: every <sequence> in the <subset> has a <convergent subsequence> whose <limit of a sequence> belongs to that <subset>. This equivalence does not extend to all <topological spaces>. For example, $x_n=1/n$ for $n\geq2$ has <limit of a sequence> zero in $[0,1]$. The same <sequence> lies in $(0,1)$, but every <subsequence> still has <limit of a sequence> zero on the <real line>, so no <subsequence> converges to a point of $(0,1)$. Merely approaching a <boundary> is harmless when the required point remains available. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-100b-analysis-i-fall-2010/b7780b2a0629359b11c5de998c72a147_MIT18_100BF10_compactness.pdf[MIT Analysis I, Compactness vs. Sequential Compactness, Theorem 1 and its metric-space proof]{ref}

\Image[compactness-missing-endpoint.png]
{title=The same <sequence> approaches an included endpoint in a <closed interval> and a missing endpoint in an <open interval>}

Both panels show the <sequence> $x_n=1/n$ for $n\geq2$ approaching zero. In the <closed interval>, zero is an allowed <limit of a sequence>; in the <open interval>, the hollow marker identifies the missing point. This single <sequence> disproves <sequential compactness> of $(0,1)$; <compactness> of $[0,1]$ still requires control of every <open cover> or every <sequence>, supplied by the <Heine-Borel theorem>.

An <infinite set> with the <discrete topology> similarly fails <compactness>, using the <open cover> by all its <singletons>. Every <finite set> is <compact> in any <topology>: select one covering <open set> for each point. The <empty set> is <compact> because the empty selection is a <finite subcover>. This <open cover> definition does not require the <topological space> to be <Hausdorff>; in <algebraic geometry> it is often called <quasi-compactness>.

The beauty of <compactness> is that information valid around each point can often be controlled using finitely many regions. A <finite subcover> lets us combine finitely many local bounds, or choose the smallest of finitely many positive tolerances, to obtain control across the whole <compact space>. This is why <compactness> repeatedly turns approximation into existence and why it is so useful in <optimization> through the <extreme value theorem>.

Every <closed set> in a <compact space> is <compact>, and a <continuous image of a compact space> is <compact>. The latter proof pulls an <open cover> back by <inverse images>, selects a <finite subcover>, and maps forward. This makes <compactness> useful for proving existence: the <extreme value theorem> guarantees that a <continuous function> from a nonempty <compact space> to the <real line> attains both a <minimum> and a <maximum>. For example, $q(x)=x(1-x)=1/4-(x-1/2)^2$ on $[0,1]$ has <maximum> $1/4$ at $1/2$ and <minimum> zero at its endpoints. On $(0,1)$ its <infimum> is still zero but is never attained. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-101-analysis-ii-fall-2005/babd982be745679b6d691f78b1c18f53_lectures.pdf[MIT Analysis II, Theorems 1.24-1.25]{ref}

= Rectangular neighborhood of a product of compact sets
{parent=Compact space}
{title2=$K\times L\subseteq U\times V\subseteq W$}

If $K$ and $L$ are <compact subsets> of two spaces and $W$ is an <open neighborhood> of their product, there are <open sets> $U,V$ with $K\times L\subseteq U\times V\subseteq W$. First use <compactness> of $L$ to produce a rectangle around each slice $\{x\}\times L$, intersecting finitely many first factors and uniting second factors. Then use <compactness> of $K$, uniting finitely many resulting first factors and intersecting second factors. No <Hausdorff> assumption is required.

= Continuous images of nested compact intersections
{parent=Compact space}
{title2=$f(\bigcap_nK_n)=\bigcap_nf(K_n)$}

Let $X$ be a <Hausdorff space>, let $K_n$ be a nested sequence of nonempty compact subsets of $X$, and let $f:X\to Y$ be a <continuous map> into a <T1 space>. Then the displayed equality holds. The forward inclusion is true for every map. For the reverse, fix $y\in\bigcap_nf(K_n)$. Each $F_n=K_n\cap f^{-1}(\{y\})$ is nonempty and closed in the compact set $K_1$: the $K_n$ are closed by the Hausdorff assumption, while the fiber is closed because $Y$ is T1 and $f$ is continuous. The nested sets $F_n$ have the <finite intersection property>, so compactness gives a point in their intersection. Its image is $y$.

The proof only needs the relevant fibers to be closed in $K_1$, rather than all of $Y$ to be T1. A <Hausdorff space> as codomain is a familiar sufficient special case. Mere continuity into an arbitrary space does not suffice.

= Failure of nested compact image intersection without closed fibers
{parent=Continuous images of nested compact intersections}

Take $X=[0,1]$ with its usual topology and $Y$ the <Sierpiński space> with open singleton $\{1\}$. The map $f(0)=0$, $f(x)=1$ for $x>0$ is continuous, since $f^{-1}(\{1\})=(0,1]$ is relatively open in $X$. The sets $K_n=[0,1/n]$ are nonempty, nested and compact, but
$$
f\!\left(\bigcap_nK_n\right)=\{0\},\qquad\bigcap_nf(K_n)=\{0,1\}.
$$
The fiber of $1$ is not closed, which is exactly where the compact-intersection proof fails. This distinguishes the valid Hausdorff-codomain theorem from a false extension to arbitrary codomains.

= Closed subset of a compact space
{parent=Compact space}
{title2=$F\text{ closed in compact }X\ \Longrightarrow\ F\text{ compact}$}

Every <closed subset> $F$ of a <compact space> $X$ is <compact> in its <subspace topology>. An <open cover> of $F$ by ambient <open sets>, together with $X\setminus F$, covers $X$. A <finite subcover> for $X$ still covers $F$ after the extra member is discarded. The result does not require $X$ to be <Hausdorff>.

= Finite intersection characterization of compactness
{parent=Compact space}
{title2=$\{F_i\}\text{ closed with the finite intersection property}\ \Longrightarrow\ \bigcap_iF_i\ne\varnothing$}

A <topological space> is <compact> exactly when every family of <closed sets> with the <finite intersection property> has nonempty <intersection>. Take <complements> and apply <De Morgan's laws>: an empty total <intersection> corresponds to an <open cover>, while an empty finite <intersection> corresponds to a <finite subcover>. This works without any <Hausdorff condition>. With the convention that an empty-family <intersection> is the whole ambient <set>, the <finite intersection property> includes nonemptiness of that ambient <set>, and the criterion correctly treats the empty <compact space>.

= Ultrafilter characterization of compactness
{parent=Compact space}

A <topological space> is <compact> if and only if every <ultrafilter> on it converges to at least one point, assuming the <ultrafilter lemma>. No <Hausdorff> assumption is needed. In a compact space, the closures of the filter members have the <finite intersection property>, so choose a point in their intersection. Every open neighbourhood of that point belongs to the ultrafilter, since otherwise its closed complement would be a filter member. Conversely, extend the complements of a cover with no finite subcover to an ultrafilter; a limit point lies in a covering open set, contradicting the simultaneous membership of that set and its complement.

= Compact exhaustion
{parent=Compact space}

A compact exhaustion of an open subset $U$ of a finite-dimensional <Euclidean space> is an increasing sequence of compact subsets $K_n\subset U$ with $K_n\subset\operatorname{int}K_{n+1}$ and $\bigcup_nK_n=U$. For example, choose smoothed bounds or suitable nested levels of distance from the complement and distance from the origin. Exit times from these sets express the lifetime of a <maximal local solution of a stochastic differential equation>; every finite-time continuous path lying in $U$ has compact image and is contained in some $K_n$.

= Compact Hausdorff space
{parent=Compact space}

A compact Hausdorff space is both <compact> and <Hausdorff>. Such a space is <normal topological space>[normal], and every real-valued <continuous function> on it is bounded.

= Connected intersection of a closed filter base
{parent=Compact Hausdorff space}
{title2=$\bigcap\mathcal F\ne\varnothing$}

A closed <filter base> in a <compact space> has nonempty <intersection>. If the space is Hausdorff and every base member is <connected>, the <intersection> is <connected>. Otherwise <normal space>[normality] separates its two closed components by disjoint <open sets>. <Compactness> of the complement supplies finitely many base members whose <intersection> lies in that <union>; directedness then gives a single base member meeting both sides, contradicting its <connected space>[connectedness].

= Supremum convergence for decreasing continuous functions
{parent=Compact Hausdorff space}
{title2=$\sup_K f_n\downarrow\sup_K\inf_n f_n$}

On a nonempty <compact Hausdorff space>, let continuous nonnegative functions decrease pointwise. Their suprema decrease to a finite number $L$. For every $t<L$, the sets $\{f_n\geq t\}$ are nonempty nested closed sets; compactness gives a common point with limit value at least $t$. Thus the supremum of the pointwise limit is $L$. The limit need not be continuous, and this conclusion does not claim uniform convergence as in the <Dini theorem>.

= Closed relation generated by a set-split pair of compact Hausdorff maps
{parent=Compact Hausdorff space}

Let continuous maps $f,g:X\rightrightarrows Y$ between <compact Hausdorff spaces> have an underlying <split coequalizer> $q:Y\to Z$ with set maps $s,t$ satisfying $qs=1$, $ft=1$ and $gt=sq$. Its equivalence relation is the image of the closed subset $\{(x,x')\in X^2:g(x)=g(x')\}$ under $(f,f)$. One inclusion follows from $qf=qg$; for the other use $x=t(y)$ and $x'=t(y')$ whenever $q(y)=q(y')$. This image is compact and hence closed in the Hausdorff product $Y^2$. The quotient is therefore compact Hausdorff, giving the preserved <coequalizer> required by the <Beck monadicity theorem>.

= Separable compactification need not be metrizable
{parent=Compact Hausdorff space}

Embed $\mathbb N$ into the compact product $\{0,1\}^{\mathcal P(\mathbb N)}$ by $j(n)_S=\mathbf1_S(n)$ and take its closure $X$. The <Tychonoff theorem> makes $X$ a <compact Hausdorff space>, while $j(\mathbb N)$ is countable, dense and discrete in its subspace topology. Yet $X$ is not metrizable. Any convergent subsequence $j(n_k)$ of distinct points would have a convergent coordinate for $S=\{n_2,n_4,\ldots\}$, whereas that coordinate alternates between zero and one. This contradicts sequential compactness of a <compact metric space>. Thus a dense metrizable subspace does not force a separable compact Hausdorff ambient space to be metrizable.

= Heine-Borel theorem
{c}
{parent=Compact space}
{wiki=Heine–Borel_theorem}
{title2=$K\subseteq\mathbb R^d:\quad K\text{ compact}\iff K\text{ closed and bounded}$}

For every finite $d\geq1$, a <subset> $K\subseteq\mathbb R^d$ of <Euclidean space> is <compact> if and only if it is a <closed set> and a <bounded set>. Here <closed> means <closed> in the ambient <Euclidean space>, not merely in $K$ itself, and the <bounded set> condition uses the <Euclidean norm>. The <empty set> is included: it is a <closed set>, a <bounded set>, and <compact>. https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_metcompact.html[Jiří Lebl, Basic Analysis, Theorem 7.4.14]{ref}

This makes an apparently infinite test practical. To verify <compactness> of a <closed interval> $[a,b]$ with finite endpoints, we need not examine every <open cover>: it is <closed> in the <real line> and a <bounded set>. By contrast, the <open interval> $(0,1)$ is a <bounded set> but is not <closed> in the <real line>, as the missing-endpoint figure in <compact space> illustrates. The whole <real line> is <closed> but is not a <bounded set>, so it too fails <compactness>. These examples show why both hypotheses are needed.

The same criterion holds in any real or complex <finite-dimensional vector space> equipped with a <norm>: all <norms> there are <equivalent norms>, so they give the same <open sets>, <closed sets>, and <bounded sets>. Since <compactness> depends only on the <topology>, choosing a different <norm> does not change the conclusion. Without an appropriate <topology>, a bare <vector space> alone has no notion of <compactness>.

The restriction to a <finite-dimensional vector space> is essential. In the <l2 sequence space>, an <infinite-dimensional vector space> that is also a <Hilbert space>, let $e_n$ be the <vectors> of its standard <orthonormal basis>. All lie in the <closed unit ball> $B=\{x:\lVert x\rVert\leq1\}$, which is a <closed set> and a <bounded set> in the <norm topology>. For distinct indices, their <inner product> is zero, giving
$$
\lVert e_n-e_m\rVert^2=\lVert e_n\rVert^2+\lVert e_m\rVert^2-2\operatorname{Re}\langle e_n,e_m\rangle=2.
$$
Thus distinct terms remain distance $\sqrt2$ apart. No <subsequence> is a <Cauchy sequence>, hence none is a <convergent sequence>. The equivalence of <compactness> and <sequential compactness> in a <metric space> proves that this <closed unit ball> is not <compact>. The conclusion concerns the <norm topology>.

A short proof outline in <Euclidean space> explains the role of a <finite-dimensional vector space>. For a <sequence> in a <bounded set>, apply the <Bolzano-Weierstrass theorem> to each of the <Cartesian coordinates> in turn, retaining a <subsequence> at each of the finitely many steps. The final <subsequence> is a <convergent sequence> in all <Cartesian coordinates>, and a <closed set> contains its <limit of a sequence>. This yields <sequential compactness>, hence <compactness>. Conversely, a <compact subset> of a <metric space> is a <closed set> and a <bounded set>. https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_metcompact.html[Lebl, Basic Analysis, proof of Theorem 7.4.14]{ref}

= Unbounded continuous witnesses to noncompactness of a real subspace
{parent=Heine-Borel theorem}

If $X\subseteq\mathbb R$ is not <compact>, there is an unbounded <continuous function> $X\to\mathbb R$. By the <Heine-Borel theorem>, either $X$ is <unbounded>, when $f(x)=x$ works, or it is not <closed>. In the latter case choose $a\in\overline X\setminus X$; the <continuous function> $f(x)=|x-a|^{-1}$ is unbounded because points of $X$ approach the missing <limit point> $a$. This proof uses the specific compactness characterization of real subspaces; boundedness of a general <metric space> does not imply compactness.

= Finite intersection property
{parent=Compact space}
{wiki}

A family of sets has the finite intersection property when every finite subfamily has nonempty intersection. A <topological space> is compact exactly when every family of closed subsets with the finite intersection property has nonempty total intersection.

= Continuous image of a compact space
{parent=Compact space}

Let $X,Y$ be <topological spaces>, let $X$ be <compact>, and let $f:X\to Y$ be a <continuous map>. Then its <image> $f(X)$ is <compact> with the <subspace topology> inherited from $Y$. More generally, a <continuous map> sends every <compact subset> $K\subseteq X$ to a <compact subset> $f(K)\subseteq Y$, by applying the same statement to its restriction to $K$. Neither $X$ nor $Y$ needs to be <Hausdorff>.<Point-Set Topology by Romyar Sharifi/Chapter 3: Connected and compact spaces>[Lemma 3.2.5 and Proposition 3.2.9]{ref}

For example, the <polynomial function> $f(x)=x^2$ sends the <compact> <closed interval> $[0,1]$ onto $[0,1]$. The <open intervals> $V_1=(-1/10,16/25)$ and $V_2=(9/25,11/10)$ give an <open cover> of the <image>. Their <inverse images> in the domain are
$$
f^{-1}(V_1)=[0,4/5),\qquad f^{-1}(V_2)=(3/5,1].
$$
Both are <open sets> in the <subspace topology> of $[0,1]$, even though neither is an <open set> in the ambient <real line>. They cover the domain, so the corresponding two target <open sets> cover the <image>.

\Image[continuous-square-compact-image-open-cover.png]
{title=An <open cover> of the <image> of a <continuous map> and its <inverse images> on a <compact> <closed interval>}

Matching colors identify the target <open sets> and their <inverse images>. Filled endpoints are included and hollow endpoints excluded. This particular <open cover> illustrates the mechanism; <compactness> requires a <finite subcover> for every <open cover>.

For the general proof, write an arbitrary <open cover> of $f(X)$ as $\{f(X)\cap V_i:i\in I\}$ with each $V_i$ an <open set> in $Y$. By <continuity>, the <inverse images> $f^{-1}(V_i)$ form an <open cover> of $X$. Its <compactness> provides indices $i_1,\ldots,i_m$ such that
$$
X=\bigcup_{j=1}^{m}f^{-1}(V_{i_j})\quad\Longrightarrow\quad f(X)\subseteq\bigcup_{j=1}^{m}V_{i_j}.
$$
This gives the required <finite subcover> of $f(X)$. The argument uses <inverse images> because <continuous maps> need not send <open sets> to <open sets>. For the <empty set>, the empty selection suffices.

The <image> of a <compact Hausdorff space> under a <continuous map> need not itself be <Hausdorff>. Take $D=\{0,1\}$ with the <discrete topology> and $S=\{0,1\}$ with the <Sierpiński space> <topology> $\{\varnothing,\{1\},S\}$. The <identity map> $D\to S$ is a <continuous> <bijection>, since the <inverse image> of every target <open set> is an <open set> in $D$. Its <image> is <compact>, but the two points of $S$ have no disjoint <open> <neighborhoods>. If the target $Y$ is <Hausdorff>, its <subspace topology> does make $f(X)$ <Hausdorff>, and the <compact subset of a Hausdorff space> theorem also makes $f(X)$ <closed> in $Y$.

The practical payoff is existence: the <extreme value theorem> uses the <compact> <image> of a nonempty <compact space> under a real-valued <continuous function> to obtain an attained <minimum> and <maximum>. When $f$ is also a <bijection> onto a <Hausdorff> target, the <compact-to-Hausdorff continuous bijection theorem> supplies <continuity> of its inverse without a separate formula.

= Quotient of a compact space
{parent=Compact space}

Every quotient of a compact space is compact because the quotient projection is continuous and surjective.

= Lebesgue number lemma
{parent=Compact space}
{c}
{wiki}

Every open cover of a compact metric space has a number $\delta>0$ such that every subset of diameter less than $\delta$ lies in one member of the cover.

= Normality of a compact Hausdorff space
{parent=Compact space}

Every compact Hausdorff space is normal: disjoint closed subsets have disjoint open neighbourhoods. Pointwise Hausdorff separation becomes uniform first over one compact closed set and then over the other by taking finite subcovers.

= Alexander's subbase lemma
{parent=Compact space}
{wiki}
{c}

= Alexander subbase theorem
{c}
{synonym}

A topological space is compact when every cover by members of a fixed subbase has a finite subcover. Equivalently, every family of complements of subbasic opens with the finite-intersection property has nonempty intersection.

= Locally compact space
{parent=Compact space}
{wiki}

= Local compactness
{synonym}

A space is locally compact when every point has a base of neighbourhoods containing compact neighbourhoods. A compact Hausdorff space is locally compact because regular separation produces $x\in V\subseteq\overline V\subseteq U$.

= Compactly detected closed-set theorem in a locally compact Hausdorff space
{parent=Locally compact space}

If $X$ is locally compact Hausdorff and $A\cap K$ is closed in every compact subset $K$, then $A$ is closed. Around each point outside $A$, use a compact neighbourhood and the relative openness of its complement of $A\cap K$.

= Hausdorff space
{parent=Topology}
{c}
{wiki}

= Hausdorff
{synonym}
{c}

= Hausdorff condition
{synonym}
{c}

A Hausdorff space separates any two distinct points by disjoint open neighborhoods.

= Completely regular Hausdorff space
{parent=Hausdorff space}

= Tychonoff space
{c}
{synonym}

A <Hausdorff space> is completely regular if a point $x$ outside a <closed set> $F$ can be separated from $F$ by a <continuous function> $u:X\to[0,1]$ with $u(x)=1$ and $u|_F=0$. These functions determine the original <topology>: for any neighborhood $O$ of $x$, choose $F=X\setminus O$ to obtain $x\in\{u>1/2\}\subseteq O$. This is exactly the point-separation property needed for the evaluation embedding into the weak-star dual of bounded continuous functions.

= Compact subset of a Hausdorff space
{parent=Hausdorff space}

Let $K\subseteq X$, where the ambient <topological space> $X$ is <Hausdorff> and $K$ is <compact> in its <subspace topology>. Then $K$ is a <closed set> in $X$: the <complement> $X\setminus K$ is an <open set> in $X$. The ambient space need not be <compact>. For example, $[0,1]$ is a <compact> <closed interval> in the non-<compact> <Hausdorff> <real line>.<Point-Set Topology by Romyar Sharifi/Chapter 3: Connected and compact spaces>[Lemma 3.2.7 and Proposition 3.2.8]{ref}

The ambient <Hausdorff> hypothesis matters even when $K$ itself is <Hausdorff>. In the <Sierpiński space> $S=\{0,1\}$, the <open sets> are $\varnothing,\{1\},S$, so the <closed sets> are $\varnothing,\{0\},S$. Thus $K=\{1\}$ is <compact> and <Hausdorff> in its one-point <subspace topology>, but is not <closed> in $S$: its <complement> $\{0\}$ is not an <open set>. Every <open> <neighborhood> of $0$ contains $1$, so separation from $K$ fails. In contrast, the <discrete topology> on the same underlying <set> is <Hausdorff> and makes both <singletons> <closed>.

\Image[sierpinski-compact-subset-closedness.png]
{title=A <compact subset> is <closed> in the two-point <discrete space> but need not be <closed> in the <Sierpiński space>}

The diagram lists every <open set> and <closed set>, so the distinction can be checked directly. All <subsets> in both panels are <compact>: for any <open cover> of a <finite set>, choose one covering <open set> per point, obtaining a <finite subcover>. <Compactness> alone therefore cannot distinguish the two panels.

Here is the finite-selection mechanism in the general proof. For $x\in X\setminus K$ and each $k\in K$, the <Hausdorff> property gives disjoint <open> <neighborhoods> $U_k$ of $k$ and $V_k$ of $x$. The $U_k$ give an ambient <open cover> of $K$. Select a <finite subcover> $U_{k_1},\ldots,U_{k_m}$ and set
$$
U=\bigcup_{j=1}^{m}U_{k_j},\qquad V=\bigcap_{j=1}^{m}V_{k_j}.
$$
Then $K\subseteq U$, $x\in V$, and $U\cap V=\varnothing$. The finite <intersection> makes $V$ an <open set>; an infinite <intersection> would not guarantee this. Thus every point of $X\setminus K$ has an <open> <neighborhood> contained in that <complement>, proving that $K$ is <closed>. The <empty set> is already <closed>.

This theorem combines with the <continuous image of a compact space> theorem to make every <continuous map> from a <compact space> to a <Hausdorff space> a <closed map>: a <closed subset> of its domain is <compact>, and its <image> is <compact> and hence <closed> in the target. This is the reason the inverse in the <compact-to-Hausdorff continuous bijection theorem> is <continuous>. The converse implication, from <closed> to <compact>, needs additional hypotheses: the <real line> is <closed> in itself but not <compact>; in a <compact space>, every <closed subset> is <compact>.

= Separation of a point and a compact set
{parent=Compact subset of a Hausdorff space}
{title2=$x\notin K\ \Longrightarrow\ x\in V,\ K\subseteq U,\ U\cap V=\varnothing$}

In a <Hausdorff space>, a point $x$ outside a <compact subset> $K$ can be separated from all of $K$ by <disjoint> <open sets>. Choose <disjoint> <open neighbourhoods> $U_k$ of $k$ and $V_k$ of $x$ for each $k\in K$. Finitely many $U_k$ cover $K$ by <compactness>. Their <union> and the matching finite <intersection> of the $V_k$ give the displayed separation. If $K$ is empty, choose $U=\varnothing$, $V=X$. Thus $X\setminus K$ is <open>, proving that $K$ is <closed>.

= Separation of disjoint compact subsets
{parent=Separation of a point and a compact set}
{title2=$P\cap Q=\varnothing\ \Longrightarrow\ P\subseteq U,\ Q\subseteq V,\ U\cap V=\varnothing$}

Two <disjoint> <compact subsets> of a <Hausdorff space> have <disjoint> <open neighbourhoods>. For every $p\in P$, <separation of a point and a compact set> gives <disjoint> <open sets> $G_p\ni p$ and $H_p\supseteq Q$. Choose finitely many $G_p$ covering $P$, then take their <union> for $U$ and the matching finite <intersection> of the $H_p$ for $V$. The <compactness> of both <subsets> is used, once in the point-versus-<compact set> result and once in the outer finite selection. The ambient <Hausdorff space> need not be <compact> or a <normal space>.

= Compact-to-Hausdorff continuous bijection theorem
{parent=Hausdorff space}

A continuous bijection from a compact space to a Hausdorff space is a homeomorphism. It maps closed sets to compact sets, which are closed in the codomain, so it is a closed map and its inverse is continuous.

= Closed diagonal theorem
{parent=Hausdorff space}
{wiki}

A space is Hausdorff exactly when its diagonal is closed in its square.

= Homeomorphism
{parent=Topology}
{wiki}
{title2=$f:X\xrightarrow{\sim}Y$}

= Homeomorphic
{synonym}

A homeomorphism is a <bijection> between <topological spaces> such that both $f$ and $f^{-1}$ are <continuous maps>. Equivalently, for every <subset> $U\subseteq X$, $U$ is an <open set> if and only if $f(U)$ is an <open set> in $Y$. A <homeomorphism> therefore transports the entire <topology>, including <connectedness> and <compactness>. It need not preserve numerical <metric> values, and a <bounded set> need not map to a <bounded set>.

For example, an explicit <homeomorphism> between an <open interval> and the <real line> is
$$
f:(0,1)\to\mathbb R,\qquad f(x)=\tan\bigl(\pi(x-1/2)\bigr),\qquad
f^{-1}(y)=\frac12+\frac{\arctan y}{\pi}.
$$
The <tangent function> on $(-\pi/2,\pi/2)$ is strictly increasing and takes every real value; the displayed <arctangent> inverse verifies the <bijection>. Both formulas define <continuous maps> on their stated domains. Computed values are $f(1/4)=-1$, $f(1/2)=0$, and $f(3/4)=1$. Points approach infinity as $x$ approaches an excluded endpoint. Thus a <bounded set> can have the same <topology> as an unbounded one; neither of these <topological spaces> is <compact>.

A useful shortcut is the <compact-to-Hausdorff continuous bijection theorem>: a <continuous> <bijection> from a <compact space> to a <Hausdorff space> is a <homeomorphism>. Its images of <closed sets> are <compact sets>, hence <closed sets> in the target, which proves <continuity> of the inverse. The <indiscrete topology> example shows why the <Hausdorff> assumption matters. https://www.maths.ox.ac.uk/system/files/attachments/Preliminary%20Reading%20%281%29.pdf[Richard Haydon, Oxford topology preliminaries, Theorem 0.3]{ref}

= Radial extension of a square boundary homeomorphism
{parent=Homeomorphism}

A <homeomorphism> $g:\partial Q\to\partial Q$ of the boundary of a closed square $Q$ extends to a <homeomorphism> of $Q$ by fixing its centre $c$ and setting $H(c+r(p-c))=c+r(g(p)-c)$ for $p\in\partial Q$ and $0<r\leq1$. Each noncentral point lies on a unique ray from $c$ meeting the boundary once, so the formula is well defined and its inverse is obtained from $g^{-1}$. Both maps are <continuous> off the centre by the radial parametrization, and at the centre because their distances from $c$ are bounded by $r$ times the radius of the square. It follows that complements of any two closed boundary arcs are <homeomorphic>: first map the arcs to one another by a boundary <homeomorphism>, then extend radially.

= Circle and interval are not homeomorphic
{parent=Homeomorphism}

The <circle> $S^1$ and the <closed interval> $[0,1]$ are both <compact> and <connected>, so those two properties alone do not distinguish them. Instead examine <connectedness> after removing a point. Every punctured <circle> is <connected>: after rotating the missing point to $(1,0)$, the <continuous map> $t\mapsto(\cos t,\sin t)$ from the <connected> <open interval> $(0,2\pi)$ maps onto it. <Continuous maps> preserve <connectedness>. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-101-analysis-ii-fall-2005/babd982be745679b6d691f78b1c18f53_lectures.pdf[MIT Analysis II, Theorem 1.29]{ref}

If a <homeomorphism> $h:S^1\to[0,1]$ existed, choose $p=h^{-1}(1/2)$. Its restriction would give a <homeomorphism> between $S^1\setminus\{p\}$ and $[0,1]\setminus\{1/2\}$. But the latter splits into two nonempty disjoint <open sets> in its <subspace topology>, $[0,1/2)$ and $(1/2,1]$, contradicting <connectedness> of the punctured <circle>. Removing an endpoint of $[0,1]$ leaves a <connected space>; the proof deliberately chooses the preimage of an interior point. This illustrates how a simple <connectedness> test can rule out every possible <homeomorphism>.

= Inverse continuity from an open bijection
{parent=Homeomorphism}

A continuous bijection that is an <open map> is a <homeomorphism>. If $g=f^{-1}$ and $U$ is open in the original domain, then $g^{-1}(U)=f(U)$ is open by the open-map property. This proves continuity of the inverse directly.

= Local homeomorphism
{parent=Homeomorphism}
{wiki}

A local homeomorphism is a continuous map $f:X\to Y$ such that every point of $X$ has a neighbourhood on which $f$ restricts to a homeomorphism onto an open subset of $Y$.

= Closed map
{parent=Homeomorphism}
{wiki}

A closed map sends closed subsets of its domain to closed subsets of its codomain. A bijection is a homeomorphism exactly when it is continuous and closed.

= Perfect map
{parent=Closed map}

A perfect map is a continuous closed map whose <fibers> are <compact>. Surjectivity is sometimes an additional convention; here it is not imposed, and empty <fibers> are <compact>. For maps between <Tychonoff spaces>, the <perfect-map characterization by Stone-Čech remainders> identifies this property using the unique extension to their compactifications.

= Perfect-map characterization by Stone-Čech remainders
{parent=Perfect map}
{title2=$f\text{ perfect}\iff(\beta f)^{-1}(Y)=X$}

For a <continuous map> $f:X\to Y$ between <Tychonoff spaces>, extend it to $F:\beta X\to\beta Y$ by the <Stone-Čech extension property>. If $f$ is perfect, <compactness> of a <fiber> and closedness of $f$ show that no remainder point can map to a point of $Y$: separate such a point from the <fiber> by a neighborhood in $\beta X$, and use closedness to obtain a target neighborhood whose preimage stays inside it, contradicting density. Conversely, if $F^{-1}(Y)=X$, each <fiber> is <compact>, and for closed $A\subseteq X$ the equality $f(A)=F(\overline A^{\beta X})\cap Y$ proves closedness. Equivalently $F(\beta X\setminus X)\subseteq\beta Y\setminus Y$.

= Projection with a compact factor is closed
{parent=Closed map}
{title2=$X\text{ compact}\implies\pi_Y:X\times Y\to Y\text{ closed}$}

For <metric spaces> $X,Y$ with $X$ <compact>, the <projection with a compact factor is closed>: if $F\subset X\times Y$ is a <closed set> and $y_n\in\pi_Y(F)$ converges to $y$, choose $x_n$ with $(x_n,y_n)\in F$. <Sequential compactness> supplies $x_{n_k}\to x$, so $(x_{n_k},y_{n_k})\to(x,y)\in F$, proving $y\in\pi_Y(F)$. The <projection map> is also <continuous> for the sum <metric>. Without <compactness>, the <closed set> $\{(x,y)\in\mathbb R^2:xy=1\}$ projects to the nonclosed set $\mathbb R\setminus\{0\}$.

= Compact-preimage theorem for closed maps
{parent=Closed map}

Let $f:X\to Y$ be a <continuous map> that is a <closed map> and whose fibres are <compact>. Then $f^{-1}(K)$ is <compact> for every <compact set> $K\subseteq Y$, without a <Hausdorff> hypothesis. Given an <open cover>, cover each fibre by a finite union $V_y$ of its members. The set $W_y=Y\setminus f(X\setminus V_y)$ is open, contains $y$, and has $f^{-1}(W_y)\subseteq V_y$. A finite subcover of $K$ by the $W_y$ uses only finitely many original cover members and covers $f^{-1}(K)$. Empty fibres cause no exception.

= Unit sphere
{title2=$S^n$}
{parent=Topology}
{wiki}

The unit sphere in $\mathbb R^{n+1}$ is
$$
S^n=\{x\in\mathbb R^{n+1}:\|x\|=1\}.
$$

= Closed graph theorem for compact spaces
{parent=Topology}
{wiki}

A map between compact Hausdorff spaces is continuous exactly when its graph is closed.

= Closed-graph criterion with compact codomain
{parent=Closed graph theorem for compact spaces}

If $X,Y$ are metric spaces, $Y$ is compact, and the graph of $f:X\to Y$ is closed, then $f$ is continuous. Any failure of sequential continuity gives a subsequence whose images stay away from the proposed limit; compactness produces a convergent image subsequence, and closedness of the graph forces its limit to be the correct value.

= Curve
{parent=Topology}
{wiki}

A curve in a <topological space> $X$ is a <continuous function> from an interval into $X$.

= Intersection curve
{parent=Curve}

= Plane curve
{parent=Curve}
{wiki}

A <plane curve> is a <curve> contained in a <plane>. In <algebraic geometry>, one studies <plane curves> defined by <polynomial> equations. General <plane curves>, such as an <Archimedean spiral>, need not be <algebraic curves>.

= Tractrix
{parent=Plane curve}
{wiki}
{title2=$r=\sin\theta,\quad h=\cos\theta+\log\tan(\theta/2)$}

A <tractrix> is a <plane curve> whose tangent segment from the point of contact to a fixed axis has constant length. For segment length one and a positive acute tangent angle $\theta$, its height is $r=\sin\theta$. The slope relation $dr/dh=\tan\theta$ gives $dh/d\theta=\cos^2\theta/\sin\theta$. Integrating yields the displayed axial coordinate up to an additive constant. Conversely these coordinates have the required slope, and their tangent intercept is at distance $r/\sin\theta=1$. Rotating this profile about its axis gives a <tractoid>.

= Trochoid
{parent=Plane curve}
{wiki}

The trajectory of a point at fixed distance $a$ from the centre of a circle of radius $r$ rolling along a line. In coordinates aligned with that line it has a linear centre displacement plus sinusoidal components. The case $a=r$ is a <cycloid>; $a<r$ gives a shortened trajectory and $a>r$ gives loops. An inclined rolling line rotates this geometry relative to horizontal and vertical coordinates.

= Vertical upward velocity of a rolling-point trochoid
{parent=Trochoid}
{title2=$a>r$}

A point at distance $a$ from the axis of a radius-$r$ cylinder rolling downhill at constant axis speed $v$ has $\dot x=v[\cos\alpha+(a/r)\cos\theta]$, $\dot y=-v[\sin\alpha+(a/r)\sin\theta]$. For $0\le\alpha<\pi/2$, the existence of $\dot x=0$, $\dot y>0$ is equivalent to $a>r$. At $a=r$ the relevant zero-horizontal-velocity point is stationary, so a point within or on the rolling cylinder never has strictly vertical upward motion.

= Hyperbolic spiral
{parent=Plane curve}
{title2=$r=a/(\theta-\theta_0)$}

A hyperbolic spiral is a <plane curve> whose radial <distance> in <polar coordinates> is inversely proportional to the angular displacement from a fixed angle: $r=a/(\theta-\theta_0)$, on a branch where $r>0$. For $a>0$ and $\theta>\theta_0$, it winds infinitely often towards the origin as $\theta\to\infty$. Its geometric winding alone does not determine whether a moving <particle> traverses it in finite time: that also requires a law for the angular <velocity>.

= Involute
{parent=Plane curve}
{wiki}

An <involute> is the curve traced by the end of a taut string as it is unwound from a fixed curve. For a <circle> of radius $R$, one parametrization is $\mathbf x(\theta)=R(\cos\theta+\theta\sin\theta,\sin\theta-\theta\cos\theta)$. The straight free <length> is $R\theta$, and differentiation shows that the traced <velocity> is perpendicular to the taut string. Reversing the motion winds the string back around the circle.

= Winding time around a circular pole
{parent=Involute}
{title2=$t_{\rm hit}=L^2/(2Rv)$}

A point <mass> attached to a light inextensible string winds around a fixed smooth circular pole of radius $R$. Let the initial straight free <length> be $L$, and assume the string remains taut without slipping over the part already laid on the pole. Since its <tension> is perpendicular to the point <mass>'s <velocity>, the <speed> $v$ stays constant. If the contact point turns through $d\theta$, the free <length> $s$ obeys $ds=-R\,d\theta$, while the <involute> geometry gives $v=s\dot\theta$. Thus $s\dot s=-Rv$, or $s^2=L^2-2Rvt$. First contact of the point <mass> with the pole occurs at $s=0$, after $L^2/(2Rv)$. In the ideal model, the required <tension> $mv^2/s$ diverges at contact; finite string strength or finite <particle> size limits that idealization.

= Lemniscate of Bernoulli
{parent=Plane curve}
{wiki}
{title2=$(x^2+y^2)^2=a^2(x^2-y^2)$}

= Bernoulli's lemniscate
{c}
{synonym}

A figure-eight <plane curve> with two congruent lobes and a crossing at the origin. With scale $a>0$, its polar equation is $r^2=a^2\cos2\theta$. One quarter has coordinates $x=at\sqrt{(1+t^2)/2}$, $y=at\sqrt{(1-t^2)/2}$, $0\leq t\leq1$. Differentiation gives arc element $a\,dt/\sqrt{1-t^4}$. The total <arc length> is therefore $4a\int_0^1dt/\sqrt{1-t^4}$.

= Astroid
{parent=Plane curve}
{title2=$|x/a|^{2/3}+|y/a|^{2/3}=1$}

An astroid is the four-cusped <plane curve> parametrized by $x=a\cos^3t$, $y=a\sin^3t$ for $a>0$. It appears as the relative trajectory in the <astroid motion of a harmonic oscillator in a rotating frame>. The parametrization has zero first derivative at the cusps while remaining smooth as a function of its parameter.

= Plane cusp of type (2,5)
{parent=Plane curve}
{title2=$x^2-y^5=0$}

This irreducible plane branch has parametrization $x=t^5$, $y=t^2$. Its only singular point is the origin. The <resolution of the (2,5) cusp by two blowups> makes its <strict transform> smooth in every characteristic.

= Resolution of the (2,5) cusp by two blowups
{parent=Plane cusp of type (2,5)}
{title2=$(x,y)=(t^5,t^2)\longmapsto(u,y)=(t^3,t^2)\longmapsto(v,y)=(t,t^2)$}

Blow up the origin and use the chart $x=uy$, giving the <strict transform> $u^2-y^3=0$. Blow up its remaining singular point and use $u=vy$, giving $v^2-y=0$, which is smooth because its derivative in $y$ is $-1$. The complementary chart at each stage has no point of the <strict transform> on its exceptional divisor. These two <blowups of a smooth algebraic surface> resolve the branch, although the smooth transform is still tangent to an exceptional divisor.

= Archimedean spiral
{c}
{parent=Plane curve}
{title2=$r=a+b\theta$}
{wiki}

= Archimedes' spiral
{c}
{synonym}

An Archimedean spiral has radius linear in polar angle, $r=a+b\theta$. For the special parametrization $(u\cos u,u\sin u)$, the <curvature of a plane curve> is $(u^2+2)/(1+u^2)^{3/2}$. Its parameter speed is $\sqrt{1+u^2}$, so it remains a <regular curve> at the origin.

= Scaled length of an Archimedean spiral
{parent=Archimedean spiral}
{title2=$\lim_{a\to0^+}aL_a=R^2/2$}

Let an <Archimedean spiral> have <polar radius> $r=at$ and <angle> $t$ in <polar coordinates>, with $0\leq t\leq R/a$, where $R>0$ is fixed. Parameterizing by $r$ gives the <arc length>
$$
aL_a=\int_0^R\sqrt{a^2+r^2}\,dr.
$$
The bounds $r\leq\sqrt{a^2+r^2}\leq r+a$ yield
$$
\frac{R^2}{2}\leq aL_a\leq\frac{R^2}{2}+aR.
$$
The <squeeze theorem> proves the displayed <limit>. Thus the <arc length> grows like $R^2/(2a)$ as the turns become closely spaced, without requiring an explicit antiderivative.

= Superelliptic curve
{parent=Plane curve}
{wiki}

A superelliptic curve has an affine equation $y^m=f(x)$. When $f$ is square-free and the characteristic does not divide $m$, projection to the $x$-coordinate is a finite morphism whose finite ramification occurs over the roots of $f$; its behavior at infinity depends on $m$ and $\deg f$.

= Canonical map of a smooth plane curve
{parent=Plane curve}

For a smooth plane curve of degree $d\geq4$, adjunction gives
$$
K_C\cong\mathcal O_C(d-3).
$$
Its canonical map is induced by the $(d-3)$rd Veronese map and is therefore an embedding.

= Semicubical parabola
{title2=$y^2=x^3$}
{parent=Plane curve}
{wiki}

A semicubical parabola is a cusped cubic curve affinely equivalent to $y^2=x^3$.

= Coordinate ring and singularity of the semicubical parabola
{parent=Semicubical parabola}

The homomorphism
$$
k[x,y]\to k[t],
\qquad x\mapsto t^2,\quad y\mapsto t^3
$$
has kernel $(y^2-x^3)$ and image $k[t^2,t^3]$. The quotient is therefore an <integral domain>, so the curve is irreducible. In characteristic zero its gradient $(-3x^2,2y)$ vanishes at the origin, making the origin its unique <singular point of an algebraic variety>.

= Euler spiral
{c}
{parent=Curve}
{wiki}

= Cornu spiral
{c}
{synonym}

The <Euler spiral> is the planar <curve> parametrized by the <Fresnel integrals>. In the optical convention,
$$
C(u)+iS(u)=\int_0^u e^{i\pi t^2/2}\,dt.
$$
The tangent has unit length and argument $\pi u^2/2$, so its signed <curvature> is $\pi u$, proportional to signed <arc length>. In <Fresnel diffraction>, an interval integral is the directed chord joining its endpoint parameters on the <Cornu spiral>. Chord length gives the interval factor's <modulus>, while chord direction gives its <complex argument>.

= Closed curve
{parent=Curve}

A <curve> $\gamma:[0,1]\to X$ whose endpoints agree, $\gamma(0)=\gamma(1)$. This is the same unbased notion as a <loop (topology)>; self-intersections are allowed.

= Asymptote
{parent=Curve}

An asymptote of an unbounded <curve> is a straight line whose distance from the curve tends to zero along a specified end. For a graph, a sloping asymptote $y=mx+b$ satisfies $y(x)-(mx+b)\to0$ as $x$ tends to the relevant infinity. Factorization of the leading quadratic part locates the two asymptotes of a nondegenerate <hyperbola>.

= Parametric curve
{parent=Curve}
{title2=$Q(t)$}

= Parametrized curve
{synonym}

A <parametric curve> is described by a vector-valued map $t\mapsto Q(t)$. Its <derivative> gives a <tangent vector> wherever it is nonzero.

// Target: geometry-and-topology.bigb

= Lissajous curve
{parent=Parametric curve}
{c}

A Lissajous curve is a <parametric curve> with coordinates $x(t)=A\sin(at+\delta)$ and $y(t)=B\sin(bt)$. The frequency ratio and phase shift control its crossings and whether its trajectory closes.

= Parametric plane curve
{parent=Parametric curve}

A <parametric curve> in the plane, given by two coordinate functions $x(t),y(t)$. Its speed is $\sqrt{x'(t)^2+y'(t)^2}$ wherever these derivatives exist, giving its <arc length> by <integration>.

= Parametric curve interrogation
{parent=Parametric curve}

A <parametric curve> interface supplies its parameter domain, evaluation, and <derivatives> where they exist. Robust geometric algorithms also need restricted subcurves and certified range or <derivative> bounds on parameter intervals. Tangent, speed and <curvature> are derived from point and <derivative> enquiries when the relevant <derivatives> are regular. Finite point samples alone cannot certify the absence of every plane intersection of an arbitrary smooth curve.

= Certified curve-plane intersection
{parent=Parametric curve interrogation}
{title2=$F(t)=n\cdot C(t)-d$}

Intersecting a <parametric curve> with a plane reduces to isolating zeros of a scalar function. A certified range for $F$ rejects intervals that do not contain zero. On an interval whose <derivative> range excludes zero, a sign-changing endpoint bracket gives exactly one root, which <bisection method> or a safeguarded <Newton root-finding iteration> refines. Other intervals are subdivided conservatively; tangencies and coplanar pieces need multiplicity or identity tests supported by the representation. An unresolved small interval is a possible contact, not a proof of an intersection. With finitely many transverse intersections and convergent enclosures, isolation terminates to any prescribed tolerance.

= Cycloid
{parent=Parametric curve}
{title2=$x=R(\theta-\sin\theta),\ y=R(1-\cos\theta)$}
{wiki}

A cycloid is the <plane curve> traced by a point on a circle rolling without slipping along a straight line. A parametrization with rolling radius $R$ is $x=R(\theta-\sin\theta)$, $y=R(1-\cos\theta)$; reversing the sign of $y$ reflects the curve. Its cusps occur at integer multiples of $2\pi$. A descending cycloidal arc solves the classical <brachistochrone problem>.

= Area and length of a cycloid arch
{parent=Cycloid}
{title2=$\mathcal A=3\pi a^2,\quad L=8a$}

For the <cycloid> $x=a(t-\sin t)$, $y=a(1-\cos t)$, $a>0$, $0\leq t\leq2\pi$, the area between the arch and its baseline is
$$
\mathcal A=\int y\,dx=a^2\int_0^{2\pi}(1-\cos t)^2dt=3\pi a^2.
$$
Its <arc length> is
$$
L=\int_0^{2\pi}\sqrt{x'(t)^2+y'(t)^2}\,dt
=2a\int_0^{2\pi}\sin(t/2)dt=8a.
$$
The positive sign of $\sin(t/2)$ inside this parameter interval removes the <absolute value> in the speed. Omitting the two endpoint cusps does not change either <integral>.

= Space curve
{parent=Curve}
{wiki}

A space curve is a <curve> in three-dimensional <Euclidean space>.

= Helix
{parent=Space curve}
{wiki}

= Helices
{synonym}

A helix is a <space curve> that winds around an axis while advancing along it at a constant rate. A circular helix can be parametrized by $(R\cos t,R\sin t,at)$.

= Logarithmic conical helix
{parent=Helix}
{title2=$x(s)=(\tfrac s2\cos\log s,\tfrac s2\sin\log s,s/\sqrt2)$}

The displayed <space curve>, $s>0$, lies on a cone and is parametrized by <arc length>. It has a constant tangent angle with its axis, while the turn spacing shrinks geometrically toward the limiting apex. Its <curvature> is $1/(\sqrt2s)$: differentiate its unit tangent with respect to $s$. Infinitely many turns accumulate at the apex with finite <arc length>; the apex itself is not a regular point of the curve.

= Pitch of a helix
{parent=Helix}
{title2=$P=2\pi\,dz/d\phi$}

= Helical pitch
{synonym}

The pitch is the axial displacement per complete turn. For a circular <helix> with $z=a\phi+b$, the signed pitch for increasing angle is $2\pi a$, and its magnitude is $2\pi|a|$. Pitch is independent of the cylinder radius.

= Inflection point
{parent=Curve}
{wiki}

An inflection point of a smooth plane curve is a point where its signed curvature changes sign. At a regular inflection point the curvature vanishes.

= Stationary inflection point
{parent=Inflection point}
{title2=$f'(a)=0$}

= Stationary point of inflexion
{synonym}

= Stationary point of inflection
{synonym}

An <inflection point> of the graph of a differentiable real <function> at which its <derivative> is zero. It has a horizontal tangent and a change of concavity, but need not be a local <maximum> or <minimum>. For a three-times continuously differentiable <function>, $f'(a)=f''(a)=0$ and $f'''(a)\ne0$ suffice: the expansion $f''(a+h)=f'''(a)h+o(h)$ gives opposite signs on the two sides. The elementary example is $f(x)=x^3$ at zero. The word “saddle” is sometimes used for this one-dimensional situation in elementary examination papers, but the usual <saddle point of a scalar function> has a multivariable meaning; do not merge the two concepts.

= Logarithmic spiral
{parent=Curve}
{wiki}

A logarithmic spiral has polar equation $r=Ae^{b\theta}$. Its tangent meets each radial line at a constant angle.

= Logarithmic spiral as an embedded real line
{parent=Logarithmic spiral}
{title2=$h(t)=e^t(\cos t,\sin t)$}

The map $h$ is a <homeomorphism> from the <real line> to its image with the <subspace topology>: its continuous inverse is $h^{-1}(x,y)=\log\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$. Different parameters have different radii, so winding does not destroy injectivity. The origin is an accumulation point of the spiral but is not a point of this space.

= Simple curve
{parent=Curve}

A simple curve is an <injective function>[injective] curve, apart from the permitted coincidence of its two endpoints when it is closed.

= Space-filling curve
{parent=Curve}
{wiki}

A space-filling curve is a <surjective function>[surjective] continuous map from an interval onto a region of dimension at least two.

\Include[topological-surface]{parent=Topology}

= Knaster-Kuratowski-Mazurkiewicz lemma
{c}
{parent=Topology}
{wiki=Knaster–Kuratowski–Mazurkiewicz_lemma}

For a simplex with vertices indexed by $0,\ldots,n$, let closed sets $F_i$ have the property that every face spanned by an index set $I$ lies in $\bigcup_{i\in I}F_i$. Then $\bigcap_{i=0}^nF_i$ is nonempty. The <triangle KKM lemma> is a two-dimensional covering formulation of this simplex-intersection principle.

= Triangle KKM lemma
{parent=Knaster-Kuratowski-Mazurkiewicz lemma}

If three closed sets cover a triangle and respectively contain its three opposite sides, then their triple intersection is nonempty.

= Distance-function barycentric map
{parent=Triangle KKM lemma}

Normalized distances to three closed sets give continuous barycentric coordinates; a covering forces at least one coordinate to vanish, placing the image on the simplex boundary.

= No-retraction covering principle
{parent=Triangle KKM lemma}

A closed-cover intersection statement for a simplex is equivalent to the nonexistence of a boundary-valued map that preserves every face.
